QB.add(42,[{"id":42001,"t":"What Is Pathology","s":"Which statement best captures the definition of pathology used in the lecture?","o":["The branch of medicine concerned with the cause, origin and nature of disease and the changes it produces","The branch of medicine concerned only with treating patients once a diagnosis has been made elsewhere","The study of normal anatomy in healthy volunteers","The study of drug doses and their side effects","The branch of medicine dealing only with surgery"],"e":"The Collins dictionary definitions given are: the branch of medicine concerned with the cause, origin and nature of disease, including the changes occurring as a result of disease; the manifestations of disease, especially changes in tissues or organs; and any variant or deviant condition from normal.","w":["Pathology investigates disease; treatment decisions draw on it but are not its definition.","Normal anatomy underpins pathology but is not pathology itself.","Drug dosing belongs to pharmacology.","Pathology is not limited to surgery."],"k":"Pathology: cause, origin, nature and changes of disease.","rn":[["","What are the 3 definitions of pathology?","What are the 3 definitions of pathology?","Branch of medicine: cause, origin, nature of disease; Manifestations of disease (tissue/organ changes); Any variant or deviant condition from normal"],["","Pathology is the branch of medicine concerned with which 3 things about disease?","Pathology is the branch of medicine concerned with which 3 things about disease?","Cause; Origin; Nature"]]},{"id":42002,"t":"What Is Pathology","s":"At which levels are pathology samples examined?","o":["Macro (by eye) and micro (cellular and molecular)","Only by eye, without a microscope","Only at the molecular level, using DNA and RNA tests in the genetics lab","Only by talking to the patient","Only by imaging such as X-ray"],"e":"In practice, samples are taken from patients and sent to laboratories for detailed examination at the macro level (what you see by eye) and the micro level (cellular and molecular). Clinical scientists and doctors are involved.","w":["Microscopy and molecular methods are also used.","Gross examination by eye is also used.","Pathology works on samples, not only history.","Imaging is not the focus described."],"k":"Pathology samples: macro and micro levels.","rn":[["","On which 2 levels are samples examined?","On which 2 levels are samples examined?","Macro; Micro (cellular and molecular)"]]},{"id":42003,"t":"What Is Pathology","s":"Why does explaining how an illness started help patient care, according to the lecture?","o":["It helps decide the best treatment, or how to manage the condition if there is none","It replaces the need to take a history","It ensures that every patient is sent for surgery as early as possible","It only matters for research, not patients","It allows the pathologist to prescribe all drugs"],"e":"Pathology explains illness at the most fundamental level and so can help plan treatment. Explaining how an illness started helps decide the best treatment or, if there is no treatment, how to manage the condition. The pathologist is the \"gatekeeper\" of information, working with other disciplines.","w":["History-taking is still essential.","Treatment depends on the diagnosis.","It directly helps patients.","Treating teams use the report to decide treatment."],"k":"Understanding cause guides treatment and management.","rn":[["","How does pathology help when a condition has no treatment?","How does pathology help when a condition has no treatment?","Helps manage the condition"],["","Why does explaining illness at the deepest level matter? (2)","Why does explaining illness at the deepest level matter? (2)","Plan treatment; Manage conditions with no treatment"]]},{"id":42004,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"A thin slice of a bowel biopsy is placed on a glass slide and stained to look at cell and tissue structure. Which specialty is this?","o":["Cellular pathology: histology","Haematology, which counts blood cells by machine","Biochemistry, which measures molecules in the blood","Immunology, which looks for misbehaving antibodies","Forensic pathology, which examines the body after death"],"e":"Cellular pathology (histology) examines the morphology of cells and tissues by cutting tissue into thin slices, putting them on a glass slide and staining them; the professional is an anatomical pathologist or histopathologist. Cytology instead smears a thick, fluid-like sample on a slide.","w":["Haematology counts and examines blood cells.","Biochemistry measures molecules in blood.","Immunology identifies misbehaving antibodies.","Forensic pathology is postmortem examination."],"k":"Histology: thin tissue slices, stained.","rn":[["","What are the 2 branches of cellular pathology?","What are the 2 branches of cellular pathology?","Histology; Cytology"]]},{"id":42005,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"A thick, fluid-like sample is simply smeared on a slide and stained. Which specialty and professional does this describe?","o":["Cytology; cytopathologist","Histology; forensic pathologist","Haematology; chemical pathologist","Biochemistry; microbiologist","Genetics; immunologist"],"e":"Cytology just smears a thick, fluid-like sample on a slide and stains it; the professional is a cytopathologist. Whether a separate cytopathologist post exists depends on the trust; generally histopathologists cover cytology too.","w":["Histology uses thin tissue slices, and forensic pathologists do postmortems.","Haematology counts blood cells; chemical pathologists work in biochemistry.","Biochemistry measures molecules; microbiologists identify microbes.","Genetics analyses DNA/RNA; immunologists study antibodies."],"k":"Cytology: smeared fluid sample (cytopathologist).","rn":[["","What does cytology do?","What does cytology do?","Smears thick fluid-like sample on slide, stains it"],["","Who works in cytology?","Who works in cytology?","Cytopathologist"]]},{"id":42006,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"A patient's liver and kidney function need monitoring through blood tests measuring specific molecules. Which specialty is involved?","o":["Biochemistry (chemical pathologist)","Haematology (haematologist)","Cytology (cytopathologist)","Forensic pathology (forensic pathologist)","Immunology (immunologist)"],"e":"Biochemistry measures molecules in the blood and is important for monitoring organ function, such as the liver and kidney. The professional is a chemical pathologist; the lecturer also mentioned clinical biochemists or chemistry consultants.","w":["Haematology counts and examines blood cells.","Cytology examines smeared fluid samples.","Forensic pathology examines the body after death.","Immunology looks for misbehaving antibodies."],"k":"Biochemistry: blood molecules, organ function.","rn":[["","Who works in biochemistry?","Who works in biochemistry?","Chemical pathologist"]]},{"id":42007,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"Which specialty identifies \"misbehaving\" antibodies, especially those that attack the body's own tissues?","o":["Immunology","Microbiology","Haematology","Histology","Forensic pathology"],"e":"Immunology identifies misbehaving antibodies, especially those that turn against our own tissues (autoimmune conditions). The lecturer noted immunology consultants are under-represented and hard to recruit (East Kent tried for two to three years).","w":["Microbiology identifies microbes and antibiotic sensitivity.","Haematology counts and examines blood cells.","Histology examines thin tissue sections.","Forensic pathology examines the dead."],"k":"Immunology: autoimmune \"misbehaving\" antibodies.","rn":[["","Immunology: when do antibodies turn against our own tissues?","Immunology: when do antibodies turn against our own tissues?","Autoimmune conditions"],["","What does immunology identify?","What does immunology identify?","\"Misbehaving\" antibodies"]]},{"id":42008,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"A patient's infection is cultured in the lab to identify the organism and which antibiotics it responds to. Which professional leads this?","o":["Microbiologist or virologist","Chemical pathologist","Cytopathologist","Forensic pathologist","Consultant in genetic pathology"],"e":"The infection specialty identifies infectious microbes by culturing them and identifies which antibiotics they respond to; the professionals are microbiologists and virologists (e.g. a consultant microbiologist).","w":["Chemical pathologists work in biochemistry.","Cytopathologists examine smeared samples.","Forensic pathologists do postmortems.","Genetic pathologists analyse DNA/RNA."],"k":"Infection: culture microbes, find antibiotic response.","rn":[["","Who works in infection pathology?","Who works in infection pathology?","Microbiologist / virologist"]]},{"id":42009,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"Which specialty is growing with advances in DNA sequencing?","o":["Genetic pathology","Forensic pathology","Cytology","Haematology","Biochemistry"],"e":"Genetic pathology uses molecular biology and genetics, analysing DNA and RNA; the professional is a consultant in genetic pathology. The lecturer noted genetic pathology (genomics) is growing with advances in DNA sequencing.","w":["Forensic pathology examines the dead.","Cytology examines smeared fluid samples.","Haematology counts blood cells.","Biochemistry measures blood molecules."],"k":"Genetic pathology: DNA/RNA analysis, growing.","rn":[["","DNA/RNA analysis belongs to which specialty?","DNA/RNA analysis belongs to which specialty?","Genetic pathology"],["","What does genetic pathology analyse?","What does genetic pathology analyse?","DNA/RNA (molecular biology/genetics)"]]},{"id":42010,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"A body is examined after death to find out why the person died, with the police involved. Which professional is this, and how do they usually train?","o":["Forensic pathologist; usually first as a general pathologist or histopathologist","Haematologist; first as a GP","Chemical pathologist; first as a surgeon","Immunologist; usually trained first as a consultant microbiologist","Cytopathologist; first as a radiologist"],"e":"Forensic pathology is the postmortem (after-death) examination of the body to find out why someone died. Forensic pathologists do autopsies, may work with the police, and usually train first as a general pathologist or histopathologist.","w":["Haematologists examine blood cells.","Chemical pathologists work in biochemistry.","Immunologists study antibodies.","Cytopathologists examine smeared samples."],"k":"Forensic pathologist: postmortem, often with police.","rn":[["","How does someone usually become a forensic pathologist?","How does someone usually become a forensic pathologist?","Pathologist / histopathologist plus further training"]]},{"id":42011,"t":"Pathology Specialties","a":["histopathologist","anatomical pathologist","histopathology"],"s":"Name the professional who examines thin stained slices of tissue to look at cell and tissue morphology.","e":"The anatomical pathologist or histopathologist examines histology: thin tissue slices on glass slides, stained. They generally also cover cytology.","k":"Histopathologist reads tissue sections.","rn":[["","Who works in histology?","Who works in histology?","Anatomical pathologist / histopathologist"],["","Which specialist usually also covers cytology work?","Which specialist usually also covers cytology work?","Histopathologist"]]},{"id":42012,"t":"Pathology Specialties","a":["morphology"],"s":"What term describes the shape and structure of cells and tissues?","e":"Morphology is shape and structure. Histology examines the morphology of cells and tissues, and haematology examines the morphology of blood cells.","k":"Morphology = shape and structure.","rn":[["","What does cellular pathology examine?","What does cellular pathology examine?","Morphology of cells and tissues"]]},{"id":42013,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"Which statement about pathologists' contact with patients is consistent with the lecture?","o":["They often do not see patients but analyse samples and inform other specialties","They always examine every patient face to face before writing a report","They make all treatment decisions alone","They only work in operating theatres","They never write reports"],"e":"Pathologists often do not see patients face to face. They analyse samples, draw conclusions and inform the other specialties, who rely on this information to make a diagnosis and decide treatment.","w":["They often do not see patients.","The treating team decides treatment using their reports.","They work in laboratories.","They report findings to other teams."],"k":"Pathologists inform others via sample analysis.","rn":[["","Why is the histopathologist important even without seeing patients?","Why is the histopathologist important even without seeing patients?","Analyses samples, informs other specialties; Others rely on it for diagnosis and treatment"]]},{"id":42014,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"Which pairing of specialty and professional is NOT correct?","o":["Haematology: chemical pathologist","Histology: histopathologist","Cytology: cytopathologist","Infection: microbiologist","Forensic pathology: forensic pathologist"],"e":"Haematology's professional is a haematologist. Chemical pathologists work in biochemistry. The other pairings are correct.","w":["Histology is read by histopathologists.","Cytology is read by cytopathologists.","Infection is the microbiologist's specialty.","Postmortems are done by forensic pathologists."],"k":"Chemical pathologists work in biochemistry, not haematology.","rn":[["","Who works in biochemistry?","Who works in biochemistry?","Chemical pathologist"]]},{"id":42015,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","s":"Put the steps of anatomical pathology in order: (P) stain thin sections on slides; (Q) examine and measure the specimen; (R) put pieces into labelled cassettes; (S) read under the microscope.","o":["Q → R → P → S","R → Q → P → S","Q → P → R → S","P → Q → R → S","S → Q → R → P"],"e":"The specimen arrives labelled with a lab number and is examined and measured, then cut up (an arrow on the paper marks orientation). Pieces go into labelled plastic cassettes; thin sections are mounted on glass slides and stained; the stained sample is examined under the microscope.","w":["The specimen must be examined before it is cut into cassettes.","Pieces go into cassettes before sectioning and staining.","Staining follows cassette processing.","Microscopy is the last step."],"k":"Specimen → cut → cassettes → stained slides → microscope.","rn":[["","What are the steps of evaluating a tissue specimen?","What are the steps of evaluating a tissue specimen?","Look at it grossly, measure; Slice thinly; Put on slides; Stain; Histopathologist analyses"],["","Anatomical pathology","Anatomical pathology","Tissue/specimen examined and cut to make slides"]]},{"id":42016,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","s":"Why must tissue sections be 10 micrometres thick or less?","o":["So single cell layers can be seen without cells overlapping","So the slide fits in the cassette","So the stain dries faster and the slide can be read on the same day","So the specimen can be weighed accurately","So microbes can be grown from it"],"e":"Sections must be 10 micrometres or less so you see single cell layers; if cells overlap it is very hard to see what is happening.","w":["Cassettes hold tissue pieces, not sections.","Drying speed is not the reason.","Weighing happens before cutting.","Culture is a microbiology process."],"k":"Sections ≤10 µm show single cell layers.","rn":[["","Why must tissue slices be so thin?","Why must tissue slices be so thin?","Single cell layers; Overlap hides what cells do"]]},{"id":42017,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","s":"Which stain is applied in every NHS pathology lab before any specialist stains?","o":["Haematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E)","Gram stain, as used for bacteria in microbiology","Ziehl-Neelsen","Silver stain","Auramine"],"e":"H&amp;E (haematoxylin and eosin) is the stain applied in every NHS pathology lab; specialist stains come afterwards to answer very specific questions.","w":["Gram stain is a microbiology stain for bacteria.","ZN is for acid-fast bacteria.","Silver stains are specialist stains.","Auramine is a fluorescent TB stain."],"k":"H&amp;E is the routine stain.","rn":[["","Which stain is used in every NHS pathology lab?","Which stain is used in every NHS pathology lab?","Haematoxylin and eosin (H&E)"],["","Image card: Which stain is this, used in every NHS pathology lab? Which specialist analyses it?","Which stain is this, used in every NHS pathology lab? Which specialist analyses it?","H&E (haematoxylin and eosin); Histopathologist"],["","H&E","H&E","Haematoxylin and eosin"]]},{"id":42018,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","s":"Who cuts and stains the tissue sections in an NHS pathology lab?","o":["Biomedical scientists, directed by the pathologist","The surgeon who took the biopsy in theatre","The patient's GP","The forensic pathologist only","The gastrointestinal consultant"],"e":"The cutting and staining is done by biomedical scientists in the NHS pathology lab, not by the pathologist, but the pathologist directs what they want.","w":["Surgeons take biopsies but do not process them.","GPs are not involved in processing.","Forensic pathologists do postmortems.","The GI consultant relies on the report."],"k":"Biomedical scientists process tissue under pathologist direction.","rn":[["","Who directs what the biomedical scientists do?","Who directs what the biomedical scientists do?","The pathologist"]]},{"id":42019,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","s":"A gastrointestinal biopsy slide is ready. Who reads it, and who uses the report?","o":["The histopathologist reads it; the GI consultant or surgeon uses the report","The GI consultant reads it; the histopathologist acts on it","The biomedical scientist reads it and decides whether the patient needs surgery","The patient reads it at home","No one reads it until autopsy"],"e":"The GI tract slide is read by the histopathologist, not the gastrointestinal consultant. The histopathologist says whether it is normal or abnormal, and the GI consultant or surgeon relies on that report to decide on treatment or surgery.","w":["The roles are reversed.","Biomedical scientists prepare slides; they do not decide on surgery.","Patients do not read slides.","Biopsies are read for living patients."],"k":"Histopathologist reports; treating team acts.","rn":[["","What does the GI consultant do with the histopathology report?","What does the GI consultant do with the histopathology report?","Decides on treatment / surgery"]]},{"id":42020,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","s":"This image shows a surgical specimen with a ruler. What does this step achieve?","o":["Gross examination and measurement of the labelled specimen","Microscopic examination of the stained cells at high power","Culture of bacteria from the tissue","Counting blood cells by machine","Measuring troponin in serum"],"e":"The specimen arrives labelled with a lab number (e.g. 22897/18) and is examined and measured, with a ruler in the photo. The gross (by-eye) look and measurement come before cutting and staining.","w":["Microscopy happens after staining.","Culture is microbiology.","Cell counting is haematology.","Troponin is biochemistry."],"k":"First step: gross examination and measurement.","f":"cl12","rn":[["","Image card: Anatomical pathology: what is the first step done with this specimen?","Anatomical pathology: what is the first step done with this specimen?","Gross examination; Measured"]]},{"id":42021,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","s":"This image shows pieces of tissue in labelled plastic holders. What are they called?","o":["Cassettes","Glass slides","Culture plates","Test tubes","Coverslips"],"e":"Different samples: pieces of the tissue are placed in labelled plastic cassettes for processing. Later, thin sections are mounted on glass slides and stained.","w":["Glass slides hold stained sections.","Culture plates are microbiology.","Test tubes hold fluids.","Coverslips cover slides."],"k":"Tissue pieces go into labelled cassettes.","f":"cl14"},{"id":42022,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","s":"These glass slides show stained sections of the specimen. What colour does the routine stain give?","o":["Pink/purple","Bright green fluorescence","Bright yellow","Black only","Colourless"],"e":"Thin sections of the tissue are mounted on glass slides and stained pink/purple; H&amp;E (haematoxylin and eosin) is the routine stain in every NHS pathology lab.","w":["Green fluorescence is auramine or immunofluorescence.","Yellow is not the H&amp;E colour.","Black-only staining is not described.","The sections are stained."],"k":"H&amp;E sections appear pink/purple.","f":"cl15"},{"id":42023,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","a":["haematoxylin and eosin","h&e","h and e","hematoxylin and eosin","h & e"],"s":"What routine stain is applied to tissue sections in every NHS pathology laboratory?","e":"Haematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E) is applied in every NHS pathology lab; specialist stains come afterwards.","k":"H&amp;E = routine stain.","rn":[["","Which stain is used in every NHS pathology lab?","Which stain is used in every NHS pathology lab?","Haematoxylin and eosin (H&E)"],["","Image card: Which stain is this, used in every NHS pathology lab? Which specialist analyses it?","Which stain is this, used in every NHS pathology lab? Which specialist analyses it?","H&E (haematoxylin and eosin); Histopathologist"],["","H&E","H&E","Haematoxylin and eosin"]]},{"id":42024,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","a":["biomedical scientist","biomedical scientists"],"s":"Who performs the cutting and staining of tissue in the NHS pathology lab under the pathologist's direction?","e":"Biomedical scientists in the NHS pathology lab cut and stain tissue, with the pathologist directing what they want.","k":"Biomedical scientists cut and stain.","rn":[["","Who directs what the biomedical scientists do?","Who directs what the biomedical scientists do?","The pathologist"],["","Who cuts and stains the slices in an NHS pathology lab?","Who cuts and stains the slices in an NHS pathology lab?","Biomedical scientists"],["","Image card: Tissue evaluation: what step is this? Who does it in an NHS lab?","Tissue evaluation: what step is this? Who does it in an NHS lab?","Stained samples on glass slides; Biomedical scientists"]]},{"id":42025,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A person dies after a recent bout of headache and feeling sick. At autopsy, yellow fluid is seen under the brain's coverings. What does this suggest?","o":["Pus, indicating infection","Normal cerebrospinal fluid","Blood from a stroke","A tumour of the meninges","Fat from a fracture"],"e":"At autopsy, the brain surface lacked its normal clear folds and showed fluid build-up. The fluid was yellow, so probably pus, and pus indicates infection. The next step is to culture some of the pus and examine it microscopically.","w":["Normal meninges are clear.","Yellow pus is not blood.","The appearance suggested infection.","Fat is not described."],"k":"Yellow fluid under meninges = pus = infection.","rn":[["","Yellow fluid under the meninges is what? What does it indicate?","Yellow fluid under the meninges is what? What does it indicate?","Pus; Infection"]]},{"id":42026,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Pus from the meninges is smeared and stained with H&amp;E. Which cell predominates, and what does it indicate?","o":["Neutrophils, indicating acute inflammation from bacterial infection","Lymphocytes, indicating a long-standing chronic viral infection of the brain","Red blood cells only, indicating bleeding","Eosinophils, indicating a parasite","Plasma cells, indicating a tumour"],"e":"Pus smeared on a slide and stained with H&amp;E showed a high concentration of neutrophils, a sign of acute inflammation (first infection). Bacterial infection leads to neutrophil infiltration, and neutrophils are very easy to see under the microscope.","w":["Lymphocytes are not the cells described.","Neutrophils, not only red cells, were seen.","Eosinophils are not described.","Plasma cells are not described."],"k":"Neutrophils in meninges = acute bacterial inflammation.","rn":[["","Neutrophils are a sign of what?","Neutrophils are a sign of what?","Acute inflammation (first infection)"],["","What causes neutrophil infiltration in acute meningitis?","What causes neutrophil infiltration in acute meningitis?","Bacterial infection"],["","Image card: Acute meningitis histopathology: which cells are infiltrating? What causes it?","Acute meningitis histopathology: which cells are infiltrating? What causes it?","Neutrophils; Bacterial infection"]]},{"id":42027,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"In this histology of the meninges, how are the neutrophils recognised at high power?","o":["By their multi-lobed nuclei among red blood cells","By their round single nuclei that fill almost the whole cell","By green fluorescence","By their acid-fast pink staining","By golden colonies"],"e":"The low-power view shows meninges packed with purple inflammatory cells; bacterial infection leads to neutrophil infiltration. At high power, neutrophils are seen with multi-lobed nuclei among red blood cells.","w":["Multi-lobed nuclei, not round ones, identify neutrophils.","Fluorescence is not used here.","Acid-fast staining is for mycobacteria.","Colonies are seen on culture plates."],"k":"Neutrophils: multi-lobed nuclei.","f":"cl20","rn":[["","Low MCV means what about the red cells?","Low MCV means what about the red cells?","Smaller red blood cells"],["","What does a haematologist focus on?","What does a haematologist focus on?","Blood; Red blood cells, white blood cells"],["","What does MCV measure?","What does MCV measure?","Size of red blood cells"]]},{"id":42028,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Put the steps that gave the meningitis diagnosis in order: (P) microbiology grows meningococci; (Q) autopsy shows pus under the meninges; (R) samples of meninges and blood are sent to microbiology; (S) histopathology shows neutrophil infiltration.","o":["Q → S → R → P","R → Q → S → P","P → Q → S → R","Q → P → R → S","S → R → Q → P"],"e":"The anatomical pathologist performs the autopsy and sees pus under the meninges, histopathology shows neutrophil infiltration, samples from the meninges and blood are sent to microbiology, which cultures them and identifies meningococci.","w":["The autopsy finding comes before samples are sent.","Growth is the final result.","Samples must be sent before organisms grow.","The autopsy comes first."],"k":"Autopsy → histology → microbiology culture → diagnosis.","rn":[["","What is the easiest next test after seeing pus at autopsy?","What is the easiest next test after seeing pus at autopsy?","Culture the pus"],["","What does microbiology do with the meningitis sample?","What does microbiology do with the meningitis sample?","Puts it on culture medium; Correct temperature and conditions"]]},{"id":42029,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","a":["meningococci","meningococcus","neisseria meningitidis"],"s":"Which bacteria grew from the meningeal and blood samples in the meningitis case?","e":"Microbiology placed a small amount of sample on a culture medium at the right temperature and conditions; bacterial colonies grew and were identified as meningococci.","k":"Case 1 organism: meningococci.","rn":[["","Case 1: which bacteria grew?","Case 1: which bacteria grew?","Meningococci"]]},{"id":42030,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which two labs together gave the diagnosis in the meningitis case?","o":["Anatomical pathology (autopsy, histopathology) and microbiology","Haematology and biochemistry","Immunology and genetics","Cytology of the fluid and forensic toxicology of the blood","Biochemistry and virology"],"e":"In case 1, the anatomical pathologist performed the autopsy and histopathology (neutrophils in the meninges), and microbiology grew meningococci. Microscopy plus the organism grown gave the diagnosis.","w":["These were not used in case 1.","These were not used in case 1.","These were not used in case 1.","These were not used in case 1."],"k":"Meningitis: histopathology + microbiology.","rn":[["","Case 1: person dies after headache and feeling sick. Who examines the body?","Case 1: person dies after headache and feeling sick. Who examines the body?","Anatomical pathologist (autopsy)"],["","Image card: Anatomical pathology: what is the first step done with this specimen?","Anatomical pathology: what is the first step done with this specimen?","Gross examination; Measured"],["","Anatomical pathology","Anatomical pathology","Tissue/specimen examined and cut to make slides"]]},{"id":42031,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A 58-year-old man has had chest pain for 24 hours. His ECG shows minor changes but not the classic changes of MI. Serum troponin I is 2000 ng/L (normal &lt;5 ng/L). What does this result indicate?","o":["Heart muscle fibres are dying and leaking their contents","The ECG is definitely normal, so the troponin can be ignored and he can go home","He has a bacterial infection of the heart","His kidneys are failing","He has iron deficiency anaemia"],"e":"Serum troponin I was sent to biochemistry: 2000 ng/L against a normal of under 5 ng/L means heart muscle fibres are dying and leaking their cell contents. He was sent for primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PPCI), the blocked artery was opened and a stent inserted, and he recovered well.","w":["A hugely raised troponin means he cannot be sent home.","Troponin indicates dying heart muscle, not infection.","Kidney function is a separate biochemistry measure.","Iron deficiency is shown by Hb and MCV."],"k":"Raised troponin = dying heart muscle.","rn":[["","What does high blood troponin mean?","What does high blood troponin mean?","Muscle fibres dying, leaking contents"]]},{"id":42032,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"What is troponin, according to the lecture?","o":["A protein in contractile muscle that stabilises actin–myosin interaction","An enzyme made only by the liver and released when it is inflamed","A hormone released by the kidneys","An antibody against bacteria","A type of white blood cell"],"e":"Troponin is a protein found in contractile muscle; it stabilises the interaction between actin and myosin. You should normally have almost none in your blood; if muscle breaks down, troponin seeps into the blood. With chest pain, a raised troponin most likely comes from dying cardiac muscle.","w":["Troponin is a muscle protein, not a liver enzyme.","It is not a kidney hormone.","It is not an antibody.","It is not a cell."],"k":"Troponin: muscle protein (actin–myosin); leaks when muscle dies.","rn":[["","What is the job of troponin?","What is the job of troponin?","Stabilises actin-myosin interaction"]]},{"id":42033,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"Which lab measured the troponin, and why is it useful clinically?","o":["Biochemistry; assays now measure specific proteins fast enough to act on","Haematology; it counts the number of dying heart cells in the blood","Microbiology; it grows the bacteria in the heart","Histopathology; it requires a heart biopsy","Immunology; it detects antibodies to heart muscle"],"e":"Serum troponin I was sent to the biochemistry lab. Troponin is measured quickly: assays can now measure specific proteins very fast, so the team can act on the result, which here led to PPCI with a stent.","w":["Haematology counts blood cells.","Microbiology identifies microbes.","Troponin is a blood test, not a biopsy.","Immunology looks for misbehaving antibodies."],"k":"Troponin: biochemistry, rapid assay.","rn":[["","What does biochemistry measure?","What does biochemistry measure?","Various molecules in the blood"]]},{"id":42034,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"After the raised troponin, what treatment did the 58-year-old receive?","o":["Primary percutaneous coronary intervention: the blocked artery was opened and a stent inserted","Antibiotics for presumed bacterial meningitis and a repeat ECG","Iron tablets for anaemia","Antiretroviral therapy for HIV","No treatment, because the ECG was not classic"],"e":"He was sent to the cardiac catheter lab for PPCI (primary percutaneous coronary intervention): the blocked artery was opened and a stent inserted. He made a good recovery and was discharged with lifestyle advice and chronic medication for ischaemic heart disease.","w":["Meningitis was case 1.","Anaemia was case 4.","HIV was case 5.","The troponin result led to treatment despite a non-classic ECG."],"k":"Raised troponin → PPCI with stent.","rn":[["","Case 2: what treatment was given?","Case 2: what treatment was given?","PPCI: open blocked artery, insert stent"],["","PPCI","PPCI","Primary percutaneous coronary intervention"]]},{"id":42035,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","a":["troponin","troponin i","troponin t"],"s":"Name the heart muscle protein measured in blood that leaks out when cardiac muscle fibres die.","e":"Troponin is a contractile muscle protein that stabilises actin–myosin interaction. A raised serum troponin with chest pain indicates dying heart muscle.","k":"Troponin = cardiac damage marker.","rn":[["","What is the job of troponin?","What is the job of troponin?","Stabilises actin-myosin interaction"],["","Case 3: what did biochemistry show?","Case 3: what did biochemistry show?","High troponin (muscle fibres dying)"],["","What does high blood troponin mean?","What does high blood troponin mean?","Muscle fibres dying, leaking contents"]]},{"id":42036,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A 50-year-old with chest pain has ST elevation on ECG and a high troponin, but dies before treatment. Autopsy shows a clot blocking a coronary artery. What does the autopsy add to the blood test?","o":["The cause: troponin showed a heart attack, the autopsy showed the blocked artery","Nothing; the blood test had already shown both the damage and its cause","It proves the death was from meningitis","It shows the patient had iron deficiency","It shows the troponin result was wrong"],"e":"Pathology can find the cause of death: troponin tells you there was a heart attack, but the autopsy shows why, a coronary artery thrombus (clot) blocking the artery. The infarcted heart muscle looks dark/black because the cells are dying (necrosis).","w":["The blood test showed damage, not its cause.","Meningitis was a different case.","Iron deficiency was a different case.","The troponin was consistent with the findings."],"k":"Troponin shows the damage; autopsy shows the cause.","rn":[["","Image card: Autopsy after a heart attack: what is blocking this artery?","Autopsy after a heart attack: what is blocking this artery?","Coronary artery thrombus"]]},{"id":42037,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"This histology shows a cross-section of a coronary artery. What is the pink mass filling the centre?","o":["A thrombus blocking blood flow","Normal blood flowing in the lumen","Pus from bacterial infection","A cluster of neutrophils","Fat in the artery wall"],"e":"The cross-section shows the ring of the artery wall; the pink mass filling the centre (lumen) is the thrombus (clot) blocking blood flow. This led to myocardial infarction in case 3.","w":["The lumen is blocked, not freely flowing.","Pus is an infection finding in case 1.","Neutrophils were the meningitis finding.","The mass fills the lumen, not the wall."],"k":"Coronary thrombus: pink clot filling the lumen.","f":"cl25","rn":[["","Image card: Autopsy after a heart attack: what is blocking this artery?","Autopsy after a heart attack: what is blocking this artery?","Coronary artery thrombus"]]},{"id":42038,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Why does infarcted heart muscle look dark at autopsy?","o":["The cells are dying (necrosis)","It is full of pus","It is stained by troponin","It contains extra iron","It is filled with viral particles"],"e":"The lecturer explained that infarcted heart muscle looks dark/black because the cells are dying (necrosis). Myocardial infarction is death of heart muscle.","w":["Pus suggests infection.","Troponin is not a stain.","Iron is not the reason given.","Viral particles are not the reason."],"k":"Infarcted muscle is dark because cells are dying.","rn":[["","Why does an infarcted area of heart look dark?","Why does an infarcted area of heart look dark?","Cells are dying (necrosis)"],["","Image card: Heart at autopsy: what is the circled area? Why does it look dark?","Heart at autopsy: what is the circled area? Why does it look dark?","Myocardial infarction; Dying cells (necrosis)"]]},{"id":42039,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","a":["thrombus","thrombosis","clot","blood clot"],"s":"What term describes a clot inside a blood vessel, such as the one blocking the coronary artery in case 3?","e":"A thrombus is a clot inside a blood vessel. In case 3 a coronary thrombus blocked the artery, causing myocardial infarction.","k":"Thrombus = intravascular clot.","rn":[["","Image card: Autopsy after a heart attack: what is blocking this artery?","Autopsy after a heart attack: what is blocking this artery?","Coronary artery thrombus"]]},{"id":42040,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","a":["myocardial infarction","mi","heart attack"],"s":"What is the term for death of heart muscle, shown at autopsy in case 3?","e":"Myocardial infarction (MI) is death of heart muscle (a heart attack). In case 3 it followed a coronary thrombus.","k":"MI = heart muscle death.","rn":[["","MI","MI","Myocardial infarction"]]},{"id":42041,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A 28-year-old woman has been tired for 3 months with heavy periods. Hb is 83 g/L (normal 115–150) and MCV 75 fL (normal 80–96). What is the most likely interpretation?","o":["Anaemia with small red cells, suggesting iron deficiency","Normal blood results","Anaemia with large red cells, because the MCV is above the normal range","A raised haemoglobin from dehydration","A heart attack shown by low MCV"],"e":"Hb 83 g/L is below normal (115–150), so she is anaemic. MCV is the size of the red cells; 75 fL is below 80–96, so the red cells are small, an indication of iron deficiency. Heavy periods lose blood and with it iron.","w":["Both values are below normal.","The MCV is low, so the cells are small, not large.","Hb is low, not raised.","MCV relates to red cell size, not the heart."],"k":"Low Hb + low MCV → ?iron deficiency anaemia.","rn":[["","Case 4: MCV result? Normal range? Suggests?","Case 4: MCV result? Normal range? Suggests?","75 fL; 80-96 fL; ?Iron deficiency"]]},{"id":42042,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which lab investigated the anaemia case?","o":["Haematology, via a full blood count","Biochemistry, via serum troponin and liver function tests","Microbiology, via blood culture","Immunology, via autoantibodies","Forensic pathology, via autopsy"],"e":"A full blood count was sent to the haematology lab, which counts blood cells using machines and examines blood cell morphology. Here the haematology lab investigated and sent the information back.","w":["Troponin is a cardiac test.","Culture identifies microbes.","Autoantibodies are immunology.","Autopsy is for the deceased."],"k":"Anaemia: haematology full blood count.","rn":[["","Case 4: which test went to which lab?","Case 4: which test went to which lab?","Full blood count; Haematology lab"]]},{"id":42043,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"What does the MCV measure?","o":["The size of the red blood cells","The number of white blood cells","The amount of troponin in the blood","The viral load in the blood","The thickness of a tissue section"],"e":"MCV (mean cell volume) is the size of the red blood cells. A low MCV means smaller red cells, an indication of iron deficiency. Hb is the haemoglobin level; low Hb means anaemia.","w":["White cell counts are separate.","Troponin is biochemistry.","Viral load is virology.","Section thickness is a histology concept."],"k":"MCV = red cell size.","rn":[["","What does MCV measure?","What does MCV measure?","Size of red blood cells"],["","Low MCV means what about the red cells?","Low MCV means what about the red cells?","Smaller red blood cells"]]},{"id":42044,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","a":["anaemia","anemia","iron deficiency anaemia","iron deficiency anemia"],"s":"What condition is defined by a haemoglobin level below normal?","e":"Anaemia is a haemoglobin (Hb) below normal. In case 4, Hb 83 g/L (normal 115–150) with a low MCV suggested iron deficiency anaemia.","k":"Low Hb = anaemia.","rn":[["","Case 4: Hb result? Normal range? Meaning?","Case 4: Hb result? Normal range? Meaning?","83 g/L; 115-150 g/L; Anaemia"]]},{"id":42045,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","a":["mean cell volume","mcv","mean corpuscular volume"],"s":"What blood index gives the average size of the red cells, low in iron deficiency?","e":"Mean cell volume (MCV) is the average red cell size; a low MCV suggests iron deficiency. Normal is 80–96 fL.","k":"MCV low → iron deficiency.","rn":[["","MCV","MCV","Mean cell volume"]]},{"id":42046,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A 42-year-old man has had repeated infections for 2 months, after unprotected sex abroad 8 months ago. Which three labs were used to confirm the diagnosis?","o":["Serology, haematology and virology","Biochemistry, histology and forensic pathology","Microbiology culture, cytology and genetics","Immunology, biochemistry and histology","Haematology alone"],"e":"Bloods were sent to the serology, haematology and virology labs. Serology looks for HIV antibodies, haematology counts CD4 T cells, and virology looks for viral particles (viral load).","w":["These labs were not used here.","These labs were not used here.","These labs were not used here.","Three labs were used."],"k":"HIV: serology, haematology, virology.","rn":[["","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","Serology: HIV antibodies; Haematology: CD4 T-cell count; Virology: viral load / particles"],["","Case 5: which 3 labs got bloods?","Case 5: which 3 labs got bloods?","Serology; Haematology; Virology"],["","Why is repeated infection with unprotected sex history a good indication of HIV?","Why is repeated infection with unprotected sex history a good indication of HIV?","Repeated infections with risk exposure suggest HIV; Confirmed by serology, haematology, virology tests"]]},{"id":42047,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"In the HIV case, what results confirmed the diagnosis, and what stage was it?","o":["Positive HIV antibodies, CD4 count 150 and high viral load: stage 4 HIV = AIDS","Negative HIV antibodies and a normal CD4 count, so HIV was excluded","Raised troponin: myocardial infarction","Low Hb and MCV: iron deficiency","Neutrophils in meninges: meningitis"],"e":"The HIV test showed positive HIV antibodies, a CD4 count of 150 (normal 430–1690) and a high viral load. The diagnosis was stage 4 HIV infection, which is AIDS.","w":["The antibodies were positive and CD4 low.","Troponin was case 2.","Hb and MCV were case 4.","Meningitis was case 1."],"k":"HIV antibodies + CD4 150 + high viral load → AIDS.","rn":[["","Case 5: what are the 3 HIV test results?","Case 5: what are the 3 HIV test results?","Positive HIV antibodies; Low CD4 count; High viral load"]]},{"id":42048,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which question does each lab answer in the HIV case?","o":["Serology: antibodies; haematology: CD4 count; virology: viral load","Serology: viral load; haematology: antibodies; virology: CD4 count","All three measure troponin","Haematology grows the virus; serology counts cells","Virology counts red cells"],"e":"Three labs, three questions: has the body made antibodies to HIV (serology), how many CD4 immune cells are left (haematology), and how much virus is in the blood (virology). Together they confirm the diagnosis.","w":["The tasks are mismatched.","Troponin is a cardiac test.","Haematology counts cells and does not grow virus.","Virology measures virus."],"k":"Serology antibodies; haematology CD4; virology viral load.","rn":[["","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","Serology: HIV antibodies; Haematology: CD4 T-cell count; Virology: viral load / particles"]]},{"id":42049,"t":"What Is Pathology","s":"Who decides which labs receive the samples in a case like this?","o":["The doctor seeing the patient","The forensic pathologist, who reviews all samples first","The patient","The biomedical scientist","The microbiology lab alone"],"e":"The lecturer stressed that the doctor seeing the patient decides which labs get samples. Travel, unprotected sex and repeated infections pointed to HIV, so samples went to serology, haematology and virology.","w":["Forensic pathologists examine the dead.","Patients do not choose labs.","Biomedical scientists process samples.","The clinician chooses the labs."],"k":"The clinician chooses which labs to use.","rn":[["","What 2 groups of healthcare workers work in pathology?","What 2 groups of healthcare workers work in pathology?","Clinical scientists; Doctors"]]},{"id":42050,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","a":["aids","acquired immunodeficiency syndrome","acquired immune deficiency syndrome"],"s":"Stage 4 HIV infection is also known as what?","e":"Stage 4 HIV infection is AIDS (acquired immunodeficiency syndrome), diagnosed in case 5 from positive antibodies, a CD4 count of 150 and high viral load.","k":"Stage 4 HIV = AIDS.","rn":[["","Case 5 diagnosis?","Case 5 diagnosis?","Stage 4 HIV infection = AIDS"]]},{"id":42051,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","a":["viral load"],"s":"What term describes the amount of virus in the blood, measured by virology?","e":"Viral load is the amount of virus in the blood. In case 5 a high viral load supported the diagnosis of AIDS.","k":"Viral load = virus in blood.","rn":[["","Case 5: what are the 3 HIV test results?","Case 5: what are the 3 HIV test results?","Positive HIV antibodies; Low CD4 count; High viral load"],["","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","Serology: HIV antibodies; Haematology: CD4 T-cell count; Virology: viral load / particles"]]},{"id":42052,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","a":["cd4 count","cd4","cd4 t cells","cd4 cell count"],"s":"Which cell count, low in advanced HIV, was measured by haematology in case 5?","e":"The CD4 count is the number of CD4 T cells; it is low in advanced HIV. In case 5 it was 150 (normal 430–1690).","k":"CD4 count low in advanced HIV.","rn":[["","Case 5: CD4 count? Normal range?","Case 5: CD4 count? Normal range?","150 µ/L; 430-1690 µ/L"],["","Case 5: what are the 3 HIV test results?","Case 5: what are the 3 HIV test results?","Positive HIV antibodies; Low CD4 count; High viral load"],["","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","Serology: HIV antibodies; Haematology: CD4 T-cell count; Virology: viral load / particles"]]},{"id":42053,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which pairing of case and key lab result is correct?","o":["Case 2 chest pain: troponin I 2000 ng/L","Case 1 meningitis: Hb 83 g/L","Case 4 tiredness: HIV antibodies positive","Case 5 infections: coronary thrombus","Case 3 death: meningococci grown"],"e":"Case 1 meningitis: neutrophils in meninges and meningococci grown. Case 2 chest pain: troponin I 2000 ng/L (normal &lt;5). Case 3 death: raised troponin, coronary thrombus, MI. Case 4 tiredness: Hb 83 g/L, MCV 75 fL. Case 5 infections: HIV antibodies positive, CD4 150, high viral load.","w":["Hb 83 was case 4.","HIV antibodies were case 5.","Coronary thrombus was case 3.","Meningococci were case 1."],"k":"Match each case to its lab and result.","rn":[["","Case 2: troponin I result? Normal?","Case 2: troponin I result? Normal?","2000 ng/L (also said as 200); <5 ng/L"]]},{"id":42054,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which case relied on BOTH biochemistry and anatomical pathology?","o":["Case 3: chest pain, death and autopsy","Case 1: meningitis, diagnosed at autopsy and by culture","Case 2: chest pain with PPCI","Case 4: tiredness and heavy periods","Case 5: repeated infections"],"e":"Case 3 used urgent biochemistry (high troponin) and then the anatomical pathologist's autopsy (coronary thrombus, myocardial infarction). Case 1 used anatomical pathology and microbiology; case 2 biochemistry only; case 4 haematology; case 5 serology, haematology and virology.","w":["Case 1 used anatomical pathology and microbiology.","Case 2 used biochemistry only.","Case 4 used haematology.","Case 5 used serology, haematology and virology."],"k":"Case 3: biochemistry + autopsy.","rn":[["","Case 3: 50-year-old man, chest pain. What did the ECG show?","Case 3: 50-year-old man, chest pain. What did the ECG show?","ST segment elevation"],["","Case 2: 58-year-old man, chest pain for 24 h. What did the ECG show?","Case 2: 58-year-old man, chest pain for 24 h. What did the ECG show?","Minor abnormal changes; Not classic MI changes"]]},{"id":42055,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which case was solved by haematology alone?","o":["Case 4: tiredness and heavy periods","Case 1: meningitis, confirmed by microbiology","Case 2: chest pain","Case 3: death after chest pain","Case 5: repeated infections"],"e":"Case 4 used a full blood count from the haematology lab (Hb 83 g/L, MCV 75 fL: ?iron deficiency anaemia). Case 5 also used haematology but alongside serology and virology.","w":["Case 1 used autopsy and microbiology.","Case 2 used biochemistry.","Case 3 used biochemistry and autopsy.","Case 5 used three labs."],"k":"Case 4: haematology alone.","rn":[["","Case 4: 28-year-old woman: what are her 2 symptoms?","Case 4: 28-year-old woman: what are her 2 symptoms?","Tired for 3 months; Heavy periods"]]},{"id":42056,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A patient's ECG is not classic for a heart attack, but the troponin is very high. How should this be interpreted, based on case 2?","o":["Heart muscle is dying even though the ECG is not clear","The troponin must be a laboratory error because the ECG is not classic","The patient must have meningitis","The patient has iron deficiency","No action is needed until the ECG changes"],"e":"In case 2 the ECG showed only minor, non-classic changes, but troponin I of 2000 ng/L showed heart muscle fibres dying. A blood test for troponin shows heart muscle is dying even when the ECG is not clear, and the patient was treated with PPCI.","w":["The result was acted upon, not dismissed.","Meningitis is unrelated.","Iron deficiency is shown by Hb and MCV.","Action was taken on the troponin."],"k":"High troponin = myocardial damage despite a non-classic ECG.","rn":[["","Case 3: what did biochemistry show?","Case 3: what did biochemistry show?","High troponin (muscle fibres dying)"],["","What does high blood troponin mean?","What does high blood troponin mean?","Muscle fibres dying, leaking contents"]]},{"id":42057,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Why does a raised troponin in a patient with chest pain most likely come from the heart, rather than other muscle?","o":["With chest pain and these symptoms, dying cardiac muscle is the most likely source","Troponin is only ever made by the heart and is absent from all other muscle","Skeletal muscle never contains troponin","Troponin comes from the liver in chest pain","Troponin is made by bacteria in the heart"],"e":"Troponin is found in contractile muscle and stabilises actin–myosin interaction. If muscle breaks down, troponin seeps into the blood. With chest pain and these symptoms, a raised troponin most likely comes from cardiac muscle dying.","w":["Troponin is in contractile muscle generally.","Troponin is found in contractile muscle.","It is not a liver protein.","It is a host muscle protein."],"k":"Chest pain + raised troponin → cardiac muscle death.","rn":[["","Case 3: what did biochemistry show?","Case 3: what did biochemistry show?","High troponin (muscle fibres dying)"],["","What does high blood troponin mean?","What does high blood troponin mean?","Muscle fibres dying, leaking contents"],["","Case 3: 50-year-old man, chest pain. What did the ECG show?","Case 3: 50-year-old man, chest pain. What did the ECG show?","ST segment elevation"]]},{"id":42058,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Heavy periods lead to tiredness in case 4. Which mechanism links them, according to the guide?","o":["Blood and iron loss reduce haemoglobin and make red cells smaller","Heavy periods raise troponin by damaging the heart muscle","Heavy periods cause bacterial meningitis","Heavy periods lower the CD4 count","Heavy periods cause a coronary thrombus"],"e":"Heavy periods lose blood and with it iron. Without enough iron the body makes too little haemoglobin and smaller red cells (low Hb, low MCV), so she feels tired.","w":["Troponin relates to muscle damage.","Meningitis is unrelated.","CD4 count relates to HIV.","Coronary thrombus relates to MI."],"k":"Blood/iron loss → low Hb and MCV → tiredness.","rn":[["","Low MCV means what about the red cells?","Low MCV means what about the red cells?","Smaller red blood cells"]]},{"id":42059,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"In the HIV case, which history features pointed the doctor towards HIV testing?","o":["Travel, unprotected sex and repeated infections","Chest pain with ST elevation on the ECG","Heavy periods and tiredness","Headache and feeling sick before death","A raised troponin"],"e":"The doctor decides which labs get samples. Travel, unprotected sex (on holiday in southern Africa 8 months earlier) and repeated infections over 2 months pointed to HIV.","w":["These suggest a cardiac cause.","These suggest anaemia.","These preceded death from meningitis.","Troponin is a cardiac marker."],"k":"History guides test choice (HIV risk factors).","rn":[["","Why is repeated infection with unprotected sex history a good indication of HIV?","Why is repeated infection with unprotected sex history a good indication of HIV?","Repeated infections with risk exposure suggest HIV; Confirmed by serology, haematology, virology tests"],["","Case 5: 42-year-old man: key symptom and history?","Case 5: 42-year-old man: key symptom and history?","Repeated infections for 2 months; Unprotected sex in southern Africa 8 months ago"]]},{"id":42060,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which statement about the five cases as a whole is correct?","o":["Each lab answered a different question in reaching the diagnosis","Only the histopathologist was needed in every one of the five cases","No case used microbiology","Every case required an autopsy","The cases show that labs are rarely useful"],"e":"The summary notes the cases show each lab answering a different question: microbiology (meningococci), biochemistry (troponin), haematology (Hb, MCV), and serology/virology (HIV), with anatomical pathology providing autopsy findings in cases 1 and 3.","w":["Several specialties were involved.","Case 1 used microbiology.","Only cases 1 and 3 had autopsies.","Labs were central to every diagnosis."],"k":"Different labs answer different diagnostic questions."},{"id":42061,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"A patient with suspected autoimmune disease needs testing for antibodies attacking their own tissues. Which lab should receive the sample?","o":["Immunology","Haematology, which counts blood cells by machine and examines their morphology","Cytology, which smears fluid samples on a slide and stains them","Forensic pathology, which examines the body after death","Biochemistry, which measures molecules for organ function"],"e":"Immunology identifies \"misbehaving\" antibodies, especially those that turn against our own tissues in autoimmune conditions. The professional is an immunologist, a specialty the lecturer described as under-represented and hard to recruit.","w":["Haematology counts and examines blood cells.","Cytology examines smeared fluid samples.","Forensic pathology is postmortem examination.","Biochemistry monitors organ function through blood molecules."],"k":"Autoimmune antibodies → immunology.","rn":[["","Immunology: when do antibodies turn against our own tissues?","Immunology: when do antibodies turn against our own tissues?","Autoimmune conditions"]]},{"id":42062,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"A wound swab is sent to find the organism and the antibiotics it responds to. Which lab?","o":["Infection (microbiology)","Genetic pathology, which analyses DNA and RNA sequences","Haematology, which counts the blood cells by machine","Chemical pathology, which measures molecules in the blood","Forensic pathology, which performs postmortem examinations"],"e":"The infection specialty identifies infectious microbes by culturing them and identifies which antibiotics they respond to (microbiologist or virologist).","w":["Genetic pathology analyses DNA/RNA.","Haematology counts blood cells.","Chemical pathology measures blood molecules.","Forensic pathology examines the dead."],"k":"Organism + antibiotic sensitivity → microbiology.","rn":[["","Culturing microbes and testing antibiotics belongs to which specialty?","Culturing microbes and testing antibiotics belongs to which specialty?","Infection (microbiology/virology)"],["","What are the 2 jobs of infection pathology?","What are the 2 jobs of infection pathology?","Identify microbes by culturing them; Find which antibiotics they respond to"]]},{"id":42063,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"A fine-needle sample of fluid from a lump is smeared on a slide for examination. Which specialty handles this?","o":["Cytology","Histology, which cuts tissue into thin slices for staining","Immunology, which looks for antibodies against self","Biochemistry, which measures serum molecules","Virology, which measures viral load in blood"],"e":"Cytology just smears a thick, fluid-like sample on a slide and stains it (cytopathologist). Histology uses thin slices of tissue.","w":["Histology uses thin tissue slices, not smears.","Immunology looks for misbehaving antibodies.","Biochemistry measures blood molecules.","Virology measures virus."],"k":"Smeared fluid sample → cytology.","rn":[["","What does cytology do?","What does cytology do?","Smears thick fluid-like sample on slide, stains it"],["","Histology vs cytology: sample","Histology vs cytology: sample","Histology: thin tissue slices; Cytology: smear of fluid-like sample"]]},{"id":42064,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A doctor wants a full blood count to assess a patient's red and white cells. Which lab?","o":["Haematology","Histology, which reads stained thin sections of tissue","Microbiology, which cultures organisms on media","Genetic pathology, which analyses DNA and RNA","Forensic pathology, which performs autopsies"],"e":"Haematology counts blood cells using machines and examines the morphology of blood cells (haematologist). A full blood count gave the Hb and MCV in the anaemia case.","w":["Histology reads tissue sections.","Microbiology cultures organisms.","Genetic pathology analyses DNA/RNA.","Forensic pathology examines the dead."],"k":"Full blood count → haematology.","rn":[["","Case 4: which test went to which lab?","Case 4: which test went to which lab?","Full blood count; Haematology lab"],["","What are the 2 jobs of haematology?","What are the 2 jobs of haematology?","Count blood cells using machines; Examine morphology of blood cells"]]},{"id":42065,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"A patient's tumour DNA is to be sequenced to look for a mutation. Which specialty?","o":["Genetic pathology","Cytology, which smears fluid on a slide for staining","Haematology, which counts blood cells by machine","Immunology, which tests for misbehaving antibodies","Microbiology, which grows microbes in culture"],"e":"Genetic pathology uses molecular biology and genetics: DNA and RNA analysis (consultant in genetic pathology). It is growing with advances in DNA sequencing.","w":["Cytology examines smeared cells.","Haematology counts blood cells.","Immunology studies antibodies.","Microbiology cultures microbes."],"k":"DNA/RNA analysis → genetic pathology.","rn":[["","DNA/RNA analysis belongs to which specialty?","DNA/RNA analysis belongs to which specialty?","Genetic pathology"],["","What does genetic pathology analyse?","What does genetic pathology analyse?","DNA/RNA (molecular biology/genetics)"]]},{"id":42066,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"A sudden unexplained death needs investigation of the cause. Which professional performs the examination?","o":["Forensic pathologist","Haematologist, who counts the blood cells by machine","Chemical pathologist, who measures molecules in serum","Cytopathologist, who examines smeared fluid samples","Immunologist, who tests for autoantibodies"],"e":"Forensic pathology is the postmortem examination of the body to find out why someone died. Forensic pathologists do autopsies, may work with the police, and usually train first as general pathologists or histopathologists.","w":["Haematologists examine blood cells.","Chemical pathologists work in biochemistry.","Cytopathologists examine smears.","Immunologists study antibodies."],"k":"Unexplained death → forensic pathologist.","rn":[["","Who works in forensic pathology?","Who works in forensic pathology?","Forensic pathologist"],["","How does someone usually become a forensic pathologist?","How does someone usually become a forensic pathologist?","Pathologist / histopathologist plus further training"],["","What does forensic pathology do?","What does forensic pathology do?","Postmortem examination to find why someone died"]]},{"id":42067,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A patient's suspected heart attack needs a rapid blood marker. Which lab measures it?","o":["Biochemistry (troponin)","Haematology (haemoglobin and the mean cell volume)","Microbiology (blood cultures on agar plates)","Histology (a biopsy of the heart muscle)","Virology (the viral load in the blood)"],"e":"Serum troponin I is measured by the biochemistry lab; a raised level shows heart muscle fibres are dying. Assays now measure specific proteins fast enough for the team to act.","w":["Hb and MCV assess anaemia.","Blood cultures identify microbes.","Troponin is a blood test, not a biopsy.","Viral load relates to viral infection."],"k":"Cardiac damage marker → biochemistry troponin.","rn":[["","Case 3: what did biochemistry show?","Case 3: what did biochemistry show?","High troponin (muscle fibres dying)"],["","Case 2: which test went to which lab?","Case 2: which test went to which lab?","Serum troponin I; Biochemistry lab"]]},{"id":42068,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"Which pairing of clinical question and lab is NOT correct?","o":["Is the patient anaemic? → microbiology","Which antibiotic works? → microbiology","Are antibodies attacking self? → immunology","How is the liver functioning? → biochemistry","Why did the person die? → forensic pathology"],"e":"Anaemia is assessed by haematology (full blood count: Hb, MCV). Microbiology identifies organisms and their antibiotic sensitivity; immunology identifies misbehaving antibodies; biochemistry monitors organ function; forensic pathology determines cause of death.","w":["Microbiology does test antibiotic response.","Immunology does look for autoantibodies.","Biochemistry does monitor liver function.","Forensic pathology does determine cause of death."],"k":"Anaemia → haematology, not microbiology.","rn":[["","Culturing microbes and testing antibiotics belongs to which specialty?","Culturing microbes and testing antibiotics belongs to which specialty?","Infection (microbiology/virology)"],["","Case 1: which samples go to microbiology?","Case 1: which samples go to microbiology?","Coverings of the brain; Blood"]]},{"id":42069,"t":"What Is Pathology","s":"The lecturer called the pathologist the \"gatekeeper\" of information. What does this describe?","o":["Working with other disciplines, asking the right questions, collecting information and solving the mystery","Preventing patients and other doctors from seeing their laboratory results","Deciding which patients may enter hospital","Controlling access to the operating theatre","Choosing which drugs a pharmacy stocks"],"e":"The pathologist is described as the \"gatekeeper\" of the information: working with other disciplines, asking the right questions, collecting the information and solving the mystery. Pathology is like being an investigator.","w":["It is not about withholding results.","It is not about hospital admissions.","It is not about theatre access.","It is not about pharmacy stock."],"k":"Pathologist as gatekeeper: collects and integrates information.","rn":[["","How does the pathologist work with other disciplines?","How does the pathologist work with other disciplines?","Requests the right information; Asks the right questions"]]},{"id":42070,"t":"What Is Pathology","s":"Why does every medical specialty have an element of pathology, according to the lecturer?","o":["Every specialist must work out what is wrong, e.g. a neurologist needs neuropathology","Every specialist must perform their own autopsies on patients who die","Every specialist runs their own laboratory","Every specialist must be a histopathologist","Pathology is only needed in surgery"],"e":"Patients tell you how something feels and what has changed; whatever specialty you follow, you then work out what is wrong and how to help, so there is always an investigative part. A GI specialist needs GI pathology; a neurologist needs neuropathology.","w":["Autopsies are done by pathologists.","Labs are run by pathology departments.","Not every specialist is a histopathologist.","Pathology spans all specialties."],"k":"All specialties need disease understanding (pathology).","rn":[["","Does every medical specialty need some pathology? Example?","Does every medical specialty need some pathology? Example?","Yes; GI specialist needs GI pathology; Neurologist needs neuropathology"]]},{"id":42071,"t":"What Is Pathology","s":"Which statement about what pathology investigates is consistent with the lecturer?","o":["How a disease started, what changes produced the symptoms, and how it differs from normal","Only the cost of treating a disease in the NHS and how to reduce it","Only how to perform surgery","Only how to take a history","Only the anatomy of healthy organs"],"e":"In the lecturer's words, pathology asks how a disease started, what changes happened at the cell and tissue level to produce the symptoms that make up the disease, and how different the situation is from normal.","w":["Cost is not the focus.","Surgery is a treatment, not pathology.","History-taking is clinical, not pathology.","Healthy anatomy underpins but is not pathology."],"k":"Pathology: origin, changes, deviation from normal.","rn":[["","What are the 3 definitions of pathology?","What are the 3 definitions of pathology?","Branch of medicine: cause, origin, nature of disease; Manifestations of disease (tissue/organ changes); Any variant or deviant condition from normal"]]},{"id":42072,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","s":"A biopsy is first looked at by eye and measured before slicing. What is this examination called?","o":["Gross (macroscopic) examination","High-power microscopy of the stained cells","Molecular analysis","Culture","Serology"],"e":"The specimen is first looked at \"gross\" (by eye) and measured, then sliced thinly and stained. Samples are examined at the macro level (by eye) and the micro level (cellular and molecular).","w":["Microscopy comes after staining.","Molecular analysis is a micro-level method.","Culture is microbiology.","Serology tests serum."],"k":"Gross = examination by eye.","rn":[["","Image card: Anatomical pathology: what is the first step done with this specimen?","Anatomical pathology: what is the first step done with this specimen?","Gross examination; Measured"]]},{"id":42073,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","s":"Why is an arrow drawn on the paper when the specimen is cut up?","o":["To mark the orientation of the tissue","To show where the patient was injured","To indicate the surgeon's name","To mark where bacteria are growing","To decorate the sample"],"e":"The tissue is cut up and an arrow drawn on the paper marks orientation, before pieces are placed in labelled cassettes.","w":["It is not an injury marker.","It does not name the surgeon.","Bacterial growth is not marked this way.","It has a practical purpose."],"k":"Arrow marks tissue orientation."},{"id":42074,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","s":"When are specialist stains used in histology?","o":["After H&amp;E, to answer very specific questions","Instead of H&amp;E, on every sample that reaches the lab","Only at autopsy","Only for blood counts","Never in NHS labs"],"e":"H&amp;E (haematoxylin and eosin) is applied in every NHS pathology lab; specialist stains come afterwards to answer very specific questions.","w":["H&amp;E is the routine first stain.","Specialist stains are used on living patients' samples too.","Blood counts are done by machine.","They are used after H&amp;E."],"k":"Specialist stains follow H&amp;E.","rn":[["","When are specialist stains used?","When are specialist stains used?","After H&E, for very specific questions"]]},{"id":42075,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"This microscope image shows the stained tissue. What is the final step it represents?","o":["Examination of the stained sample under the microscope","Gross measurement of the specimen with a ruler on arrival","Placing tissue pieces into cassettes","Culturing bacteria from the tissue","Labelling the specimen with a lab number"],"e":"The stained sample is examined under the microscope; the image shows the folded surface lining and the tissue underneath. This is the last step after examining, cutting, cassette processing, sectioning and staining.","w":["Measurement happens first.","Cassettes come before sectioning.","Culture is a microbiology process.","Labelling happens on arrival."],"k":"Final step: microscopy of the stained section.","f":"cl16","rn":[["","What does cytology do?","What does cytology do?","Smears thick fluid-like sample on slide, stains it"]]},{"id":42076,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","a":["cassette","cassettes"],"s":"Into what labelled plastic holders are pieces of tissue placed for processing?","e":"Different pieces of the tissue are placed in labelled plastic cassettes for processing, before thin sections are cut and stained.","k":"Tissue pieces go into cassettes."},{"id":42077,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","a":["gross","macroscopic","gross examination","macroscopic examination"],"s":"What term describes examination of a specimen by eye, before microscopy?","e":"Gross (macroscopic) examination is looking at the specimen by eye and measuring it, before it is sliced and stained for microscopy.","k":"Gross = by eye.","rn":[["","Image card: Anatomical pathology: what is the first step done with this specimen?","Anatomical pathology: what is the first step done with this specimen?","Gross examination; Measured"]]},{"id":42078,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A patient's haemoglobin is 140 g/L and MCV 88 fL. Using the normal ranges from the anaemia case, how should these be interpreted?","o":["Both are within normal range","Both are low, suggesting iron deficiency","Hb is below normal but the MCV is within the normal range","Hb is normal but MCV is low","Both are high, suggesting heart damage"],"e":"The normal ranges given are Hb 115–150 g/L and MCV 80–96 fL. Hb 140 and MCV 88 are both within normal range, unlike case 4 (Hb 83, MCV 75), which suggested iron deficiency anaemia.","w":["Both values are within range.","Hb 140 is within 115–150.","MCV 88 is within 80–96.","Hb and MCV do not assess heart damage."],"k":"Normal: Hb 115–150 g/L; MCV 80–96 fL.","rn":[["","Case 4: MCV result? Normal range? Suggests?","Case 4: MCV result? Normal range? Suggests?","75 fL; 80-96 fL; ?Iron deficiency"],["","Case 4: Hb result? Normal range? Meaning?","Case 4: Hb result? Normal range? Meaning?","83 g/L; 115-150 g/L; Anaemia"]]},{"id":42079,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A troponin I result is 3 ng/L. Using the normal range from case 2, how is this interpreted?","o":["Normal (below 5 ng/L)","Raised, showing heart muscle is dying","Low, showing anaemia","Raised, showing meningitis","Uninterpretable without an autopsy to look at the heart directly"],"e":"The normal troponin given in case 2 is under 5 ng/L. A result of 3 ng/L is normal. In case 2, 2000 ng/L showed heart muscle fibres dying.","w":["3 is below the 5 ng/L threshold.","Troponin does not assess anaemia.","Troponin does not diagnose meningitis.","A blood result is interpretable directly."],"k":"Normal troponin: <5 ng/L.","rn":[["","Case 2: troponin I result? Normal?","Case 2: troponin I result? Normal?","2000 ng/L (also said as 200); <5 ng/L"]]},{"id":42080,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A patient has Hb 95 g/L and MCV 72 fL. Which interpretation follows the reasoning in case 4?","o":["Anaemia with small red cells, raising the question of iron deficiency","Normal results","Anaemia with large red cells, because the MCV is above normal","High haemoglobin","Heart muscle damage"],"e":"Hb below 115 g/L means anaemia, and MCV below 80 fL means small red cells, an indication of iron deficiency, as in case 4 (Hb 83, MCV 75).","w":["Both values are below normal.","The MCV is low, not high.","The Hb is low.","Hb and MCV do not measure heart damage."],"k":"Low Hb + low MCV = microcytic anaemia (?iron).","rn":[["","Case 4: MCV result? Normal range? Suggests?","Case 4: MCV result? Normal range? Suggests?","75 fL; 80-96 fL; ?Iron deficiency"],["","Low MCV means what about the red cells?","Low MCV means what about the red cells?","Smaller red blood cells"]]},{"id":42081,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A patient's CD4 count is 900, within the normal range given in case 5. How does this compare with the patient in case 5?","o":["It is normal, whereas case 5 had a very low count of 150","It is lower than case 5","It proves stage 4 HIV infection (AIDS)","It measures viral load","It measures antibodies"],"e":"The normal CD4 range given is 430–1690. A count of 900 is normal. In case 5, a CD4 count of 150 with positive HIV antibodies and a high viral load indicated stage 4 HIV (AIDS).","w":["900 is much higher than 150.","A normal CD4 does not indicate AIDS.","Viral load is a separate virology test.","Antibodies are a separate serology test."],"k":"Normal CD4: 430–1690.","rn":[["","Case 5: CD4 count? Normal range?","Case 5: CD4 count? Normal range?","150 µ/L; 430-1690 µ/L"]]},{"id":42082,"t":"Pathology Specialties","a":["haematology","hematology"],"s":"Which lab counts blood cells using machines and examines their morphology?","e":"Haematology counts blood cells using machines and examines the morphology of blood cells; the professional is a haematologist.","k":"Haematology: blood cell counts and morphology.","rn":[["","What are the 2 jobs of haematology?","What are the 2 jobs of haematology?","Count blood cells using machines; Examine morphology of blood cells"]]},{"id":42083,"t":"Pathology Specialties","a":["biochemistry","chemical pathology","clinical biochemistry"],"s":"Which lab measures molecules in the blood and monitors organ function such as liver and kidney?","e":"Biochemistry (chemical pathology) measures molecules in the blood and is important for monitoring organ function, such as the liver and kidney.","k":"Biochemistry: blood molecules, organ function.","rn":[["","Why are blood molecules measured in biochemistry?","Why are blood molecules measured in biochemistry?","Monitor function of organs (liver, kidney)"]]},{"id":42084,"t":"Pathology Specialties","a":["immunology"],"s":"Which lab identifies antibodies that turn against the body's own tissues?","e":"Immunology identifies misbehaving antibodies, especially in autoimmune conditions.","k":"Immunology: autoimmune antibodies.","rn":[["","Immunology: when do antibodies turn against our own tissues?","Immunology: when do antibodies turn against our own tissues?","Autoimmune conditions"],["","What does immunology identify?","What does immunology identify?","\"Misbehaving\" antibodies"],["","Finding autoimmune antibodies belongs to which specialty?","Finding autoimmune antibodies belongs to which specialty?","Immunology"]]},{"id":42085,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","a":["serology"],"s":"Which lab tests blood serum, for example for HIV antibodies?","e":"Serology tests blood serum, for example for antibodies; in case 5 it detected positive HIV antibodies.","k":"Serology: antibodies in serum.","rn":[["","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","Serology: HIV antibodies; Haematology: CD4 T-cell count; Virology: viral load / particles"],["","Case 5: which 3 labs got bloods?","Case 5: which 3 labs got bloods?","Serology; Haematology; Virology"]]},{"id":42086,"t":"What Is Pathology","s":"Which healthcare workers are involved in examining pathology samples?","o":["Clinical scientists and doctors","Only nurses","Only surgeons who removed the tissue in theatre","Only patients","Only pharmacists"],"e":"Samples are taken from patients and sent to laboratories, where clinical scientists and doctors examine them at the macro and micro levels. Biomedical scientists cut and stain tissue under the pathologist's direction.","w":["Nurses are not described as the examiners.","Surgeons take samples but do not examine them in the lab.","Patients do not examine samples.","Pharmacists are not described here."],"k":"Clinical scientists and doctors examine samples.","rn":[["","What 2 groups of healthcare workers work in pathology?","What 2 groups of healthcare workers work in pathology?","Clinical scientists; Doctors"]]},{"id":42087,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"Which statement about histopathologists covering cytology is correct?","o":["Generally histopathologists cover cytology; separate posts depend on the trust","Histopathologists never look at cytology samples, only whole tissue","Cytology is always done by haematologists","Every trust has many cytopathologists","Cytology is done by surgeons"],"e":"A histopathologist looks at both cellular samples and whole tissue samples. A separate cytopathologist post depends on what each trust advertises; generally histopathologists cover cytology too.","w":["They generally do cover cytology.","Haematologists examine blood cells.","Separate posts depend on the trust.","Surgeons do not report cytology."],"k":"Histopathologists usually cover cytology.","rn":[["","Is cytopathologist usually a separate job?","Is cytopathologist usually a separate job?","Not usually; Histopathologists generally cover it"]]},{"id":42088,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"Which specialty did the lecturer describe as under-represented and hard to recruit?","o":["Immunology","Histopathology","Haematology","Biochemistry","Microbiology"],"e":"Immunology consultants are an under-represented area and hard to recruit; East Kent tried for two to three years.","w":["Histopathology was not described this way.","Haematology was not described this way.","Biochemistry was not described this way.","Microbiology was not described this way."],"k":"Immunology consultants are hard to recruit.","rn":[["","Which pathology specialty is underrepresented, with consultants hard to find?","Which pathology specialty is underrepresented, with consultants hard to find?","Immunology"]]},{"id":42089,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"A patient has fever and a suspected bacterial blood infection. Which lab can identify the organism and guide antibiotic choice?","o":["Microbiology","Histology","Genetic pathology","Haematology alone","Forensic pathology"],"e":"The infection (microbiology) lab identifies infectious microbes by culturing them and identifies which antibiotics they respond to.","w":["Histology examines tissue morphology.","Genetic pathology analyses DNA/RNA, not routine culture.","Haematology counts cells but does not culture organisms.","Forensic pathology examines the dead."],"k":"Infection + antibiotic choice → microbiology.","rn":[["","Culturing microbes and testing antibiotics belongs to which specialty?","Culturing microbes and testing antibiotics belongs to which specialty?","Infection (microbiology/virology)"]]},{"id":42090,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A doctor needs to know how much HIV is in a patient's blood to monitor disease. Which lab?","o":["Virology","Histology","Cytology","Biochemistry","Forensic pathology"],"e":"In case 5, virology looked for viral particles (viral load), serology for HIV antibodies and haematology for CD4 cell counts.","w":["Histology examines tissue.","Cytology examines smeared cells.","Biochemistry measures blood molecules such as troponin.","Forensic pathology examines the dead."],"k":"Viral load → virology.","rn":[["","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","Serology: HIV antibodies; Haematology: CD4 T-cell count; Virology: viral load / particles"]]},{"id":42091,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A patient dies suddenly after a short illness with headache and vomiting. Which sequence would best establish the cause, following case 1?","o":["Autopsy, histology, then microbiology culture","A full blood count alone, read by the haematology laboratory","A serum troponin alone, measured by the biochemistry laboratory","A DNA sequencing panel alone, run by the genetics laboratory","An HIV antibody test alone, run by the serology laboratory"],"e":"In case 1 an autopsy by the anatomical pathologist showed pus under the meninges; histopathology showed neutrophil infiltration; and samples sent to microbiology grew meningococci. Microscopy plus the organism grown gave the diagnosis.","w":["A blood count alone would not show meningeal infection.","Troponin assesses heart muscle damage.","DNA sequencing was not used here.","HIV testing was not relevant to this case."],"k":"Sudden infectious death: autopsy + histology + culture.","rn":[["","What is the easiest next test after seeing pus at autopsy?","What is the easiest next test after seeing pus at autopsy?","Culture the pus"],["","What does microbiology do with the meningitis sample?","What does microbiology do with the meningitis sample?","Puts it on culture medium; Correct temperature and conditions"]]},{"id":42092,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which statement about neutrophils in pathology samples is correct?","o":["They indicate acute inflammation","They indicate chronic viral infection lasting many years in the host","They show that a patient has iron deficiency anaemia","They show that heart muscle fibres are dying and leaking troponin","They are invisible under the light microscope without special stains"],"e":"In case 1, pus smeared and stained with H&amp;E showed a high concentration of neutrophils, a sign of acute inflammation (first infection); bacterial infection leads to neutrophil infiltration. Neutrophils are very easy to see under the microscope.","w":["Neutrophils signal acute, not chronic, inflammation here.","Iron deficiency is shown by Hb and MCV.","Heart muscle death is shown by troponin.","Neutrophils are easy to see on H&amp;E."],"k":"Neutrophils = acute inflammation.","rn":[["","Neutrophils are a sign of what?","Neutrophils are a sign of what?","Acute inflammation (first infection)"]]},{"id":42093,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"What makes pus a useful sample, according to the lecturer's reasoning?","o":["It can be cultured to identify the organism","It contains troponin that measures heart muscle damage in the patient","It contains the haemoglobin needed to diagnose anaemia in the lab","It shows the patient's CD4 count and the stage of HIV infection","It can be used to measure the patient's kidney and liver function"],"e":"Pus indicates infection. The easiest next step is to take some pus, put it on a specific culture medium and see what grows, to identify the microorganism. Smeared and stained, it also shows neutrophils.","w":["Troponin is measured in serum.","Haemoglobin is measured in a blood count.","CD4 is counted by haematology in blood.","Organ function is a biochemistry blood test."],"k":"Pus → culture to identify organism.","rn":[["","What are the 2 jobs of infection pathology?","What are the 2 jobs of infection pathology?","Identify microbes by culturing them; Find which antibiotics they respond to"]]},{"id":42094,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"In case 3, which ECG finding was present, unlike case 2?","o":["ST segment elevation","A completely normal ECG trace with no changes at all","Minor non-classic changes only, like the patient in case 2","An ECG that showed iron deficiency anaemia","An ECG that showed bacterial meningitis"],"e":"Case 2 had some minor abnormal changes but not the classic changes of MI. Case 3 had an abnormal ECG with ST segment elevation and a high troponin; he died before life-saving intervention. ECG detail comes in a later module.","w":["Case 3's ECG was abnormal.","Minor non-classic changes were case 2.","An ECG does not show anaemia.","An ECG does not diagnose meningitis."],"k":"Case 3: ST elevation; case 2: non-classic changes.","rn":[["","Case 3: 50-year-old man, chest pain. What did the ECG show?","Case 3: 50-year-old man, chest pain. What did the ECG show?","ST segment elevation"]]},{"id":42095,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts cases 2 and 3?","o":["Both had raised troponin; case 2 survived with PPCI, case 3 died and had an autopsy","Both died and had autopsies performed by the anatomical pathologist","Neither had a raised troponin on the biochemistry blood test","Case 2 had meningitis while case 3 had iron deficiency anaemia","Case 2 had an autopsy while case 3 received PPCI with a stent"],"e":"Case 2 (58-year-old) had a troponin of 2000 ng/L and was treated with PPCI and a stent, making a good recovery. Case 3 (50-year-old) had ST elevation and high troponin but died before intervention; the autopsy showed a coronary thrombus and myocardial infarction.","w":["Case 2 survived.","Both had raised troponin.","These were cardiac cases.","This reverses the outcomes."],"k":"Case 2: PPCI and recovery; case 3: death and autopsy.","rn":[["","Case 3: why was no life-saving intervention done?","Case 3: why was no life-saving intervention done?","He died before it could be done"],["","Case 2: what treatment was given?","Case 2: what treatment was given?","PPCI: open blocked artery, insert stent"],["","Case 2: troponin I result? Normal?","Case 2: troponin I result? Normal?","2000 ng/L (also said as 200); <5 ng/L"]]},{"id":42096,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which statement about PPCI is consistent with case 2?","o":["The blocked artery is opened and a stent inserted","The patient's meninges are drained of pus in theatre","The patient receives iron tablets for anaemia","The patient starts antiretroviral drugs for HIV","The heart is examined at autopsy by a pathologist"],"e":"PPCI (primary percutaneous coronary intervention) took place in the cardiac catheter lab: the blocked artery was opened and a stent inserted. He was discharged with lifestyle advice and chronic medication for ischaemic heart disease.","w":["Meningeal drainage is not described.","Iron relates to anaemia.","Antiretrovirals are not discussed.","Autopsy is for the deceased."],"k":"PPCI: open blocked coronary artery + stent.","rn":[["","Case 2: what treatment was given?","Case 2: what treatment was given?","PPCI: open blocked artery, insert stent"]]},{"id":42097,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"Which of the following is NOT a pathology specialty listed in the lecture?","o":["Radiology","Haematology, which counts and examines blood cells","Biochemistry, which measures molecules in the blood","Immunology, which looks for misbehaving antibodies","Forensic pathology, which examines the body after death"],"e":"The listed specialties: cellular pathology (histology, cytology), haematology, biochemistry, infection (microbiology/virology), immunology, genetic pathology and forensic pathology. Radiology is not listed.","w":["Haematology is listed.","Biochemistry is listed.","Immunology is listed.","Forensic pathology is listed."],"k":"Radiology is not a pathology specialty here."},{"id":42098,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"A haematologist examines a blood film. Besides counting cells by machine, what else does haematology do?","o":["Examines blood cell morphology","Grows bacteria from the blood sample on culture plates","Measures troponin to detect damage to heart muscle","Performs a postmortem to find out why someone died","Sequences the DNA of a tumour to find mutations"],"e":"Haematology counts blood cells using machines and examines the morphology (shape and structure) of blood cells.","w":["Culture is microbiology.","Troponin is biochemistry.","Postmortems are forensic pathology.","DNA sequencing is genetic pathology."],"k":"Haematology: counts and morphology of blood cells.","rn":[["","What are the 2 jobs of haematology?","What are the 2 jobs of haematology?","Count blood cells using machines; Examine morphology of blood cells"]]},{"id":42099,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"What is the main difference between histology and cytology samples?","o":["Histology uses thin tissue slices; cytology smears fluid","Histology is always done after death while cytology is only done in living patients","Cytology uses thin slices of tissue while histology smears fluid on the slide","Histology measures molecules in the blood while cytology counts the cells","There is no difference between the two kinds of sample at all"],"e":"Histology cuts tissue into thin slices, puts them on a glass slide and stains them (histopathologist). Cytology just smears a thick, fluid-like sample on a slide and stains it (cytopathologist).","w":["Both are used in living patients.","This reverses them.","Molecules are measured by biochemistry.","They differ in sample preparation."],"k":"Histology: thin slices; cytology: smears.","rn":[["","Histology vs cytology: sample","Histology vs cytology: sample","Histology: thin tissue slices; Cytology: smear of fluid-like sample"]]},{"id":42100,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A biomedical scientist accidentally cuts a section 50 micrometres thick. What problem will the pathologist face?","o":["Overlapping cells make it hard to see","The section will be too thin to stain properly with H&amp;E","The section can no longer be placed in a plastic cassette","Bacteria will grow on the slide before it can be read","The troponin level in the tissue cannot be measured"],"e":"Sections must be 10 micrometres or less so you see single cell layers; if cells overlap it is very hard to see what is happening. A 50 µm section is far too thick.","w":["The problem is excess thickness, not thinness.","Cassettes hold tissue pieces before sectioning.","Bacterial growth is not the issue.","Troponin is a blood test."],"k":"Thick sections → overlapping cells."},{"id":42101,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which statement about the macro level of pathology examination is correct?","o":["It is what you see by eye","It requires an electron microscope to see the individual cells","It is the analysis of DNA and RNA molecules in the sample","It is the counting of blood cells by a machine in the lab","It is the culturing of microbes on agar plates"],"e":"Samples are examined at the macro level (what you see by eye) and the micro level (cellular and molecular).","w":["The macro level needs no microscope.","DNA/RNA analysis is micro level.","Machine counting is haematology.","Culture is microbiology."],"k":"Macro = by eye.","rn":[["","Case 1: what do you see if you stain pus with H&E (haematoxylin and eosin)?","Case 1: what do you see if you stain pus with H&E (haematoxylin and eosin)?","High concentration of neutrophils; Possibly the microorganism"]]},{"id":42102,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"In case 1, the normal brain surface has clear folds. What did the abnormal brain show?","o":["Fluid build-up obscuring the folds","A clot blocking a coronary artery on the brain surface","Small red cells due to iron deficiency","Orange discolouration from drug treatment","Completely normal clear meninges"],"e":"At autopsy the brain surface lacked its normal clear folds and showed a fluid build-up. The fluid was yellow, so probably pus, indicating infection, compared with a normal brain with clear meninges.","w":["Coronary thrombus was case 3.","Small red cells relate to anaemia.","Orange discolouration is not described.","The meninges were abnormal."],"k":"Meningitis: pus obscuring brain surface."},{"id":42103,"t":"What Is Pathology","s":"Why is a raised troponin alone not enough to explain WHY a patient had a heart attack?","o":["It shows damage, not the cause","It is not raised in heart attacks at all, only in meningitis","It is measured only after death at autopsy","It measures red blood cell size rather than muscle damage","It measures antibodies against heart muscle"],"e":"Troponin tells you there was a heart attack (heart muscle fibres dying and leaking), but the autopsy in case 3 showed why: a coronary artery thrombus. Pathology can find the cause of death.","w":["It is raised in heart attacks.","It is a blood test in living patients.","MCV measures red cell size.","Autoantibodies are immunology."],"k":"Troponin = damage; autopsy = cause.","rn":[["","Pathology is the branch of medicine concerned with which 3 things about disease?","Pathology is the branch of medicine concerned with which 3 things about disease?","Cause; Origin; Nature"],["","Besides cause, origin and nature, what else does pathology study?","Besides cause, origin and nature, what else does pathology study?","Changes occurring as a result of disease"],["","What are the 3 definitions of pathology?","What are the 3 definitions of pathology?","Branch of medicine: cause, origin, nature of disease; Manifestations of disease (tissue/organ changes); Any variant or deviant condition from normal"]]},{"id":42104,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which lab result in case 5 came from virology?","o":["High viral load","Positive HIV antibodies detected in the serum by serology","A CD4 count of 150 measured by the haematology laboratory","A haemoglobin of 83 g/L from the full blood count","A troponin of 2000 ng/L from the biochemistry laboratory"],"e":"In case 5 virology looked for viral particles (viral load), serology for HIV antibodies and haematology for CD4 counts.","w":["Antibodies were serology.","CD4 count was haematology.","Hb was case 4.","Troponin was case 2."],"k":"Virology: viral load.","rn":[["","Case 5: what are the 3 HIV test results?","Case 5: what are the 3 HIV test results?","Positive HIV antibodies; Low CD4 count; High viral load"],["","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","Serology: HIV antibodies; Haematology: CD4 T-cell count; Virology: viral load / particles"]]},{"id":42105,"t":"Pathology Specialties","a":["histology","histopathology","cellular pathology"],"s":"Which specialty cuts tissue into thin slices, places them on glass slides and stains them?","e":"Histology (cellular pathology) examines cell and tissue morphology on thin, stained slices of tissue.","k":"Histology: thin stained tissue slices.","rn":[["","What are the 3 steps of histology?","What are the 3 steps of histology?","Cut tissue in thin slices; Put on glass slide; Stain it"],["","Histology vs cytology: sample","Histology vs cytology: sample","Histology: thin tissue slices; Cytology: smear of fluid-like sample"]]},{"id":42106,"t":"Pathology Specialties","a":["cytology","cytopathology"],"s":"Which specialty smears a thick, fluid-like sample on a slide and stains it?","e":"Cytology smears a fluid-like sample on a slide and stains it; the professional is a cytopathologist.","k":"Cytology: smears.","rn":[["","What does cytology do?","What does cytology do?","Smears thick fluid-like sample on slide, stains it"]]},{"id":42107,"t":"Pathology Specialties","a":["forensic pathology","forensic pathologist","forensic"],"s":"Which specialty performs postmortem examination to find out why someone died?","e":"Forensic pathology examines the body after death to find out why someone died; forensic pathologists may work with the police.","k":"Forensic pathology: cause of death.","rn":[["","What does forensic pathology do?","What does forensic pathology do?","Postmortem examination to find why someone died"],["","Who works in forensic pathology?","Who works in forensic pathology?","Forensic pathologist"]]},{"id":42108,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","a":["autopsy","postmortem","post-mortem","post mortem"],"s":"What is the examination of the body after death called?","e":"An autopsy (postmortem) is examination of the body after death. In cases 1 and 3 autopsies by the anatomical pathologist revealed meningitis and a coronary thrombus.","k":"Autopsy = postmortem examination.","rn":[["","Case 1: person dies after headache and feeling sick. Who examines the body?","Case 1: person dies after headache and feeling sick. Who examines the body?","Anatomical pathologist (autopsy)"]]},{"id":42109,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","a":["meninges"],"s":"What is the name for the coverings of the brain, where pus was found in case 1?","e":"The meninges are the coverings of the brain. In case 1, pus under the meninges and neutrophil infiltration indicated bacterial meningitis.","k":"Meninges = brain coverings.","rn":[["","Acute meningitis vs normal brain: meninges","Acute meningitis vs normal brain: meninges","Meningitis: pus under meninges; Normal: clear meninges"],["","Image card: Autopsy brains: which is acute meningitis? How can you tell?","Autopsy brains: which is acute meningitis? How can you tell?","Left: pus under meninges; Right: normal, clear meninges"]]},{"id":42110,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","a":["neutrophils","neutrophil"],"s":"Which white cells infiltrate tissue in acute bacterial infection and are easy to see with multi-lobed nuclei?","e":"Neutrophils infiltrate tissues in bacterial infection, a sign of acute inflammation; they have multi-lobed nuclei and are easy to see under the microscope.","k":"Neutrophils: acute bacterial inflammation.","rn":[["","Neutrophils are a sign of what?","Neutrophils are a sign of what?","Acute inflammation (first infection)"]]},{"id":42111,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","s":"A surgeon removes a bowel tumour. Who determines whether it is normal or abnormal tissue, and how is this used?","o":["The histopathologist reports; the surgeon uses the report","The surgeon reads the slides and makes the diagnosis themselves","The haematologist counts the tumour cells using a machine","The biomedical scientist makes the final diagnosis on the slide","The forensic pathologist examines the tumour after the patient's death"],"e":"The histopathologist reads the slide and says whether it is normal or abnormal; the GI consultant or surgeon relies on that report to decide on treatment or surgery. Biomedical scientists cut and stain under the pathologist's direction.","w":["The surgeon relies on the histopathologist's report.","Haematology counts blood cells.","Biomedical scientists prepare slides; they do not make the diagnosis.","This is a living patient's specimen."],"k":"Histopathologist reports; surgeon acts."},{"id":42112,"t":"What Is Pathology","s":"Which statement about \"any variant or deviant condition from normal\" is correct?","o":["It is one of the dictionary definitions of pathology","It is the definition of a forensic autopsy performed after death","It is the definition of the haematoxylin and eosin stain","It describes the normal troponin range of under 5 ng/L","It describes how a cytology sample is smeared on a slide"],"e":"The three Collins dictionary definitions of pathology: the cause, origin and nature of disease including changes from disease; the manifestations of disease, especially changes in tissues or organs; and any variant or deviant condition from normal.","w":["Autopsy is a procedure, not this definition.","H&amp;E is a stain.","The troponin range is a lab value.","Smearing is cytology technique."],"k":"Pathology includes any deviation from normal.","rn":[["","What are the 3 definitions of pathology?","What are the 3 definitions of pathology?","Branch of medicine: cause, origin, nature of disease; Manifestations of disease (tissue/organ changes); Any variant or deviant condition from normal"]]},{"id":42113,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","s":"A patient's specimen arrives labelled 22897/18. What is this?","o":["The lab number used to track the specimen","The patient's haemoglobin result in grams per litre","The thickness of the tissue section in micrometres","The number of bacterial colonies grown in culture","The patient's CD4 count in cells per microlitre"],"e":"The specimen arrives labelled with a lab number (e.g. 22897/18) and is examined and measured. Pieces then go into labelled cassettes.","w":["It is not a blood result.","Sections are 10 µm or less.","It is not a colony count.","It is not a CD4 count."],"k":"Specimens are tracked by lab number.","rn":[["","Which stain is used in every NHS pathology lab?","Which stain is used in every NHS pathology lab?","Haematoxylin and eosin (H&E)"],["","Image card: Which stain is this, used in every NHS pathology lab? Which specialist analyses it?","Which stain is this, used in every NHS pathology lab? Which specialist analyses it?","H&E (haematoxylin and eosin); Histopathologist"]]},{"id":42114,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which of the following would a chemical pathologist be LEAST likely to report?","o":["Neutrophils in the meninges","Serum troponin in a patient with chest pain","Markers of liver function in a jaundiced patient","Markers of kidney function in a patient on long-term drugs","Molecules measured in a patient's blood sample"],"e":"Chemical pathology (biochemistry) measures molecules in the blood and monitors organ function such as liver and kidney; troponin was measured by biochemistry. Neutrophils in the meninges were seen by histopathology at autopsy.","w":["Troponin is a biochemistry test.","Liver function is monitored by biochemistry.","Kidney function is monitored by biochemistry.","Blood molecules are biochemistry's work."],"k":"Tissue histology is not chemical pathology.","rn":[["","Acute meningitis vs normal brain: meninges","Acute meningitis vs normal brain: meninges","Meningitis: pus under meninges; Normal: clear meninges"],["","Image card: Autopsy brains: which is acute meningitis? How can you tell?","Autopsy brains: which is acute meningitis? How can you tell?","Left: pus under meninges; Right: normal, clear meninges"]]},{"id":42115,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"Which statement best summarises why a doctor needs pathology results in the five cases?","o":["They confirm the diagnosis and guide treatment","They replace the need for any history or examination of the patient","They are only useful for medical research, not for treatment","They are only needed after the patient has died","They are only needed for patients with infections"],"e":"Across the cases, lab results confirmed the diagnosis (meningitis, MI, iron deficiency anaemia, AIDS) and guided treatment, such as PPCI after a raised troponin. Pathology explains illness at its deepest level, helping plan treatment.","w":["History still guides which tests to send.","They directly guided treatment.","Living patients benefited (cases 2, 4, 5).","Cardiac and haematology cases were non-infectious."],"k":"Pathology confirms diagnosis and guides treatment.","rn":[["","Why is the histopathologist important even without seeing patients?","Why is the histopathologist important even without seeing patients?","Analyses samples, informs other specialties; Others rely on it for diagnosis and treatment"]]},{"id":42116,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which result pattern would most likely prompt a doctor to suspect iron deficiency?","o":["Low Hb and low MCV","High troponin with a normal blood count","Positive HIV antibodies with a low CD4 count","Neutrophils in the meninges at autopsy","A coronary thrombus seen on histology"],"e":"A low Hb means anaemia and a low MCV means small red cells, an indication of iron deficiency, as in case 4.","w":["High troponin indicates heart muscle damage.","This indicates HIV.","This indicates meningitis.","This indicates MI."],"k":"Low Hb + low MCV → iron deficiency.","rn":[["","Low MCV means what about the red cells?","Low MCV means what about the red cells?","Smaller red blood cells"]]},{"id":42117,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"Which professional generally trains first as a general pathologist or histopathologist?","o":["Forensic pathologist","Haematologist","Immunologist","Microbiologist","Chemical pathologist"],"e":"Forensic pathologists do postmortem examinations (autopsies), may work with the police, and usually train first as a general pathologist or histopathologist.","w":["This training route is not described for haematologists.","This is not described for immunologists.","This is not described for microbiologists.","This is not described for chemical pathologists."],"k":"Forensic pathologists train via histopathology.","rn":[["","Who works in forensic pathology?","Who works in forensic pathology?","Forensic pathologist"],["","How does someone usually become a forensic pathologist?","How does someone usually become a forensic pathologist?","Pathologist / histopathologist plus further training"]]},{"id":42118,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which statement about the stained GI tract slide shown in the lecture is correct?","o":["It shows a folded surface lining and tissue beneath","It shows bacterial colonies growing on an agar plate","It shows a blood film with red cells and platelets only","It shows a clot blocking a coronary artery","It shows pus under the meninges of the brain"],"e":"The stained tissue under the microscope shows the folded surface lining and the tissue underneath; it is from the GI tract and is read by the histopathologist.","w":["Colonies are seen on culture plates.","A blood film is haematology.","The coronary thrombus is a different slide.","Pus under the meninges is case 1."],"k":"GI slide: folded lining and underlying tissue."},{"id":42119,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Which combination of findings would most strongly support bacterial meningitis at autopsy?","o":["Pus under the meninges, neutrophils, and meningococci on culture","Raised troponin and a coronary thrombus at autopsy","Low Hb and low MCV on a full blood count","Positive HIV antibodies and a high viral load","A normal brain with clear meninges"],"e":"Case 1: pus under the meninges at autopsy, neutrophil infiltration on histopathology and meningococci grown by microbiology together gave the diagnosis.","w":["These indicate MI.","These indicate iron deficiency anaemia.","These indicate HIV.","Normal findings would not support meningitis."],"k":"Meningitis: pus + neutrophils + organism grown.","rn":[["","Acute meningitis vs normal brain: meninges","Acute meningitis vs normal brain: meninges","Meningitis: pus under meninges; Normal: clear meninges"],["","What is the easiest next test after seeing pus at autopsy?","What is the easiest next test after seeing pus at autopsy?","Culture the pus"],["","Image card: Autopsy brains: which is acute meningitis? How can you tell?","Autopsy brains: which is acute meningitis? How can you tell?","Left: pus under meninges; Right: normal, clear meninges"]]},{"id":42120,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"Pathology explains illness \"at the most fundamental level\". Which example from the lecture best illustrates this?","o":["Neutrophils and meningococci explaining a death","A patient describing how their headache feels","A GP deciding when to see a patient again","A pharmacist dispensing tablets","A patient choosing which hospital to attend"],"e":"Pathology examines samples down to the cellular and molecular level to explain illness and plan treatment. In case 1, histology (neutrophil infiltration) and microbiology (meningococci) explained the death at the level of cells and organisms.","w":["Symptoms start the process but are not the fundamental explanation.","Scheduling is not pathology.","Dispensing is not pathology.","Hospital choice is not pathology."],"k":"Pathology explains disease at the cell/organism level."},{"id":42121,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A 58-year-old man has had chest pain for 24 hours. His ECG shows only minor abnormal changes, not the classic changes of a heart attack. Serum troponin I is 2000 ng/L (normal &lt; 5 ng/L).<br><br>What does the troponin result show, and what is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Heart muscle fibres are dying; refer for PPCI to open the artery","The ECG is normal enough to rule out heart damage; discharge him home","Skeletal muscle is breaking down from exercise; advise rest","He has iron deficiency; send a full blood count","He has a bacterial infection; send blood cultures to microbiology"],"e":"Step 1: troponin is a contractile muscle protein that stabilises actin–myosin interaction; normally there is almost none in the blood. With chest pain, a raised troponin most likely means cardiac muscle fibres are dying and leaking their contents, even when the ECG is not classic. Step 2: he is sent for primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PPCI) to open the blocked artery and insert a stent.","w":["The troponin shows heart muscle is dying despite the unclear ECG.","With chest pain, raised troponin most likely comes from heart muscle.","Iron deficiency is assessed by Hb and MCV, not troponin.","Troponin indicates muscle death, not infection."],"k":"Raised troponin with chest pain = dying heart muscle → PPCI.","rn":[["","What does high blood troponin mean?","What does high blood troponin mean?","Muscle fibres dying, leaking contents"],["","Case 3: what did biochemistry show?","Case 3: what did biochemistry show?","High troponin (muscle fibres dying)"],["","Case 2: what treatment was given?","Case 2: what treatment was given?","PPCI: open blocked artery, insert stent"]]},{"id":42122,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A 28-year-old woman has felt very tired for 3 months and has heavy periods. Full blood count: Hb 83 g/L (normal 115–150), MCV 75 fL (normal 80–96).<br><br>Which lab handles this, and what is the most likely interpretation?","o":["Haematology; anaemia with small red cells, suggesting iron deficiency","Biochemistry; liver failure causing large red cells and tiredness","Microbiology; a bacterial infection of the blood detected by culture on agar","Immunology; autoantibodies attacking the red cells","Haematology; normal results, as both values are within range"],"e":"Step 1: a full blood count goes to haematology, which counts and examines blood cells. Step 2: Hb 83 g/L is below normal (anaemia), and MCV 75 fL is below normal, meaning smaller red cells, an indication of iron deficiency, fitting blood (and iron) loss from heavy periods.","w":["A full blood count is a haematology test, and her MCV is low.","No infection is suggested.","The pattern given points to iron deficiency, not autoimmunity.","Both values are below normal."],"k":"Low Hb + low MCV → ?iron deficiency anaemia (haematology).","rn":[["","Case 4: MCV result? Normal range? Suggests?","Case 4: MCV result? Normal range? Suggests?","75 fL; 80-96 fL; ?Iron deficiency"]]},{"id":42123,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A 42-year-old man has had repeated infections over 2 months. Eight months ago he had unprotected sex on holiday in southern Africa. Blood is sent to three labs. Results: HIV antibodies positive; CD4 count 150 (normal 430–1690); high viral load.<br><br>Which pairing of lab and result is correct, and what is the diagnosis?","o":["Serology: antibodies; haematology: CD4 count; virology: viral load; AIDS","Biochemistry: antibodies; immunology: CD4; microbiology: viral load; early HIV","Haematology: antibodies; serology: viral load; virology: CD4; no HIV","Virology: CD4 count; serology: viral load; haematology: antibodies; AIDS","Microbiology: culture; biochemistry: CD4; virology: troponin; AIDS"],"e":"Step 1: serology looks for HIV antibodies, haematology counts cells (CD4 T cells) and virology looks for viral particles (viral load). Step 2: positive antibodies, a CD4 count of 150 (far below normal) and a high viral load confirm stage 4 HIV infection, which is AIDS.","w":["Antibodies are tested by serology; this is advanced, not early, disease.","The lab assignments are wrong, and HIV is confirmed.","The lab assignments are wrong.","HIV is not cultured, and troponin is unrelated."],"k":"HIV: serology (antibodies), haematology (CD4), virology (viral load).","rn":[["","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","HIV workup: what does each lab look for?","Serology: HIV antibodies; Haematology: CD4 T-cell count; Virology: viral load / particles"]]},{"id":42124,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A young adult dies after a short illness with headache and feeling sick. At autopsy, the brain surface lacks its normal clear appearance and has yellow fluid under the meninges. A smear of the fluid, stained with H&amp;E, shows many multi-lobed white cells.<br><br>What is the most likely process, and what is the best next step to name the cause?","o":["Acute bacterial inflammation; culture the pus and blood in microbiology","Chronic viral inflammation; count CD4 cells in haematology","Haemorrhage from a coronary thrombus; measure troponin","Normal post-mortem change; no further tests are needed","Autoimmune disease; send serum to immunology to look for misbehaving antibodies"],"e":"Step 1: yellow fluid under the meninges is probably pus, which indicates infection; many neutrophils (multi-lobed nuclei) on the smear show acute inflammation from bacterial infection. Step 2: samples from the meninges and blood are sent to microbiology, cultured, and the colonies identified (meningococci in the lecture case). Microscopy plus the organism grown give the diagnosis.","w":["Neutrophils indicate acute (bacterial) inflammation.","Coronary thrombus causes MI, not pus in the meninges.","Pus and neutrophils are abnormal.","Neutrophil-rich pus points to bacterial infection."],"k":"Pus + neutrophils → acute bacterial inflammation → culture.","rn":[["","Neutrophils are a sign of what?","Neutrophils are a sign of what?","Acute inflammation (first infection)"],["","Acute meningitis vs normal brain: meninges","Acute meningitis vs normal brain: meninges","Meningitis: pus under meninges; Normal: clear meninges"],["","What is the easiest next test after seeing pus at autopsy?","What is the easiest next test after seeing pus at autopsy?","Culture the pus"]]},{"id":42125,"t":"Illustrative Clinical Cases","s":"A 50-year-old man has chest pain with ST segment elevation on ECG, and urgent tests show high troponin. He dies before any intervention. At autopsy, a pink mass fills the lumen of a coronary artery, and an area of the heart muscle downstream looks dark.<br><br>What do the autopsy findings add to the blood test result?","o":["They show the cause: a coronary thrombus causing myocardial infarction","They show the troponin was a lab error","They show the cause was infection of the heart with meningococci grown at autopsy","They show iron deficiency anaemia","They show the heart muscle was normal"],"e":"Step 1: raised troponin shows heart muscle fibres were dying (a heart attack). Step 2: the autopsy shows why: a coronary artery thrombus (clot) blocking blood flow, and myocardial infarction (dead heart muscle, dark because the cells are dying: necrosis). Pathology can find the cause of death.","w":["The autopsy confirms heart muscle death.","There is no infection described.","Anaemia is a haematology finding unrelated to this.","Dark heart muscle indicates dying cells."],"k":"Troponin shows damage; autopsy shows the cause (thrombus → MI).","rn":[["","Image card: Heart at autopsy: what is the circled area? Why does it look dark?","Heart at autopsy: what is the circled area? Why does it look dark?","Myocardial infarction; Dying cells (necrosis)"],["","Image card: Autopsy after a heart attack: what is blocking this artery?","Autopsy after a heart attack: what is blocking this artery?","Coronary artery thrombus"],["","MI","MI","Myocardial infarction"]]},{"id":42126,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"A 60-year-old has several samples taken. Sample A is thin slices of a bowel biopsy stained on glass slides. Sample B is a thick, fluid-like sample smeared directly onto a slide. Sample C is blood sent to check liver and kidney function.<br><br>Which specialties examine samples A, B and C?","o":["Histology, cytology, biochemistry","Cytology, histology, haematology","Histology, haematology, microbiology","Biochemistry, cytology, immunology","Forensic pathology, cytology, genetic pathology"],"e":"Step 1: cellular pathology covers histology (tissue cut into thin slices, put on a slide and stained: a histopathologist) and cytology (a thick, fluid-like sample smeared on a slide and stained: a cytopathologist). Step 2: biochemistry measures molecules in the blood and is important to monitor organ function such as liver and kidney.","w":["A and B are reversed, and organ function is biochemistry.","Fluid smears are cytology, and organ function is biochemistry.","Tissue slices are histology.","The biopsy is histology and the blood is biochemistry."],"k":"Histology = slices; cytology = smears; biochemistry = blood molecules.","rn":[["","Histology vs cytology: sample","Histology vs cytology: sample","Histology: thin tissue slices; Cytology: smear of fluid-like sample"]]},{"id":42127,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","s":"A surgical specimen arrives in the pathology lab. A biomedical scientist accidentally cuts sections 40 micrometres thick instead of the usual thickness. The histopathologist finds the cells overlap so much it is very hard to see what is happening.<br><br>What section thickness is needed, and why?","o":["10 micrometres or less, so single cell layers can be seen","At least 100 micrometres, so whole organs can be seen","Exactly 40 micrometres, so overlapping cells can be compared","Any thickness, as staining corrects for overlap","Sections are not needed; whole specimens are stained"],"e":"Step 1: after gross examination and measuring, tissue is cut, put into cassettes, sliced thinly onto glass slides and stained (H&amp;E first, then specialist stains). Step 2: sections must be 10 micrometres or less so that single cell layers are seen; if cells overlap it is very hard to see what is happening.","w":["Thick sections make cells overlap.","40 micrometres is too thick.","Staining does not correct overlap.","Thin sections are cut and stained."],"k":"Sections ≤10 µm show single cell layers.","rn":[["","Why must tissue slices be so thin?","Why must tissue slices be so thin?","Single cell layers; Overlap hides what cells do"]]},{"id":42128,"t":"Evaluating a Tissue Sample","s":"A gastroenterologist takes a biopsy from a patient's gut. The biopsy is processed in the lab and examined. The gastroenterologist then decides whether the patient needs surgery.<br><br>Who reads the slide, and how is the result used?","o":["The histopathologist reads it and reports normal or abnormal to the treating team","The gastroenterologist reads it, as GI specialists always read their own gut biopsy slides","The biomedical scientist reads it and decides on surgery","The microbiologist reads it to choose an antibiotic","The forensic pathologist reads it as part of a postmortem"],"e":"Step 1: biomedical scientists cut and stain the tissue, directed by the pathologist, and the histopathologist reads the slide and says whether it is normal or abnormal. Step 2: pathologists often do not see patients; the gastrointestinal consultant or surgeon relies on the report to decide on treatment or surgery.","w":["The slide is read by the histopathologist.","Biomedical scientists prepare the slides but do not decide treatment.","Microbiologists identify microbes and antibiotic sensitivity.","Forensic pathologists perform postmortems."],"k":"Histopathologist reads; treating team decides.","rn":[["","What does the histopathologist tell the consultant about a slide?","What does the histopathologist tell the consultant about a slide?","Whether it is normal or abnormal"],["","What does the GI consultant do with the histopathology report?","What does the GI consultant do with the histopathology report?","Decides on treatment / surgery"]]},{"id":42129,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"A 35-year-old woman has joint pain and a rash. Her doctor suspects her immune system is producing antibodies against her own tissues. Blood is sent to a specialist lab.<br><br>Which specialty is most appropriate, and what will it look for?","o":["Immunology; “misbehaving” antibodies against her own tissues","Haematology; the number and shape of her red and white blood cells","Biochemistry; liver and kidney function molecules","Microbiology; organisms grown in culture","Genetic pathology; DNA and RNA sequences"],"e":"Step 1: autoimmune conditions involve antibodies turning against our own tissues. Step 2: the immunology lab identifies “misbehaving” antibodies, especially those against self tissues (autoimmune conditions), and is run by an immunologist.","w":["Haematology counts and examines blood cells.","Biochemistry measures blood molecules for organ function.","Microbiology identifies microbes.","Genetic pathology analyses DNA/RNA."],"k":"Immunology lab: autoantibodies.","rn":[["","Immunology: when do antibodies turn against our own tissues?","Immunology: when do antibodies turn against our own tissues?","Autoimmune conditions"]]},{"id":42130,"t":"Pathology Specialties","s":"Three patients are seen in clinic: patient 1 has a fever and a wound infection; patient 2 died suddenly and the police ask why; patient 3 has a suspected inherited condition.<br><br>Which specialties are best for patients 1, 2 and 3?","o":["Microbiology; forensic pathology; genetic pathology","Biochemistry; cytology; haematology","Forensic pathology; microbiology; immunology","Genetic pathology; histology; microbiology","Haematology; immunology; forensic pathology"],"e":"Step 1: infection specialists (microbiologists/virologists) identify microbes by culture and their antibiotic sensitivity; forensic pathologists perform postmortems to find out why someone died, may work with the police, and usually first train as general or histopathologists. Step 2: genetic pathology analyses DNA/RNA, a growing field with advances in sequencing.","w":["These do not match the clinical questions.","The first two are swapped.","The order is wrong.","These do not match the clinical questions."],"k":"Infection → microbiology; death → forensic; inherited → genetic.","rn":[["","What does forensic pathology do?","What does forensic pathology do?","Postmortem examination to find why someone died"],["","Who works in forensic pathology?","Who works in forensic pathology?","Forensic pathologist"],["","Who works in genetic pathology?","Who works in genetic pathology?","Consultant in genetic pathology"]]}]);
